525
00:22:01,750 --> 00:22:05,350
many firms are creating such value-driven products?

526
00:22:05,630 --> 00:22:09,070
but we believe we have truly focused on this very aspect.

527
00:22:09,070 --> 00:22:11,423
We are surely the first in the industry

528
00:22:11,423 --> 00:22:14,019
to scale value-driven products effectively.

529
00:22:14,340 --> 00:22:17,000
for instance, our value products at AppLovin

530
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,660
account for most of the company's ad spend.

531
00:22:20,190 --> 00:22:21,812
this is because our value model

532
00:22:21,812 --> 00:22:23,750
is the finest in the entire industry.

533
00:22:24,430 --> 00:22:27,961
and what ties into this value model is that, as an advertiser,

534
00:22:27,961 --> 00:22:31,119
you care about how much value it can generate for you

535
00:22:31,119 --> 00:22:32,730
over a long period of time

536
00:22:32,730 --> 00:22:35,610
once I run this ad, will I break even within a year,

537
00:22:35,610 --> 00:22:38,490
or even double my returns by then, or maybe in two?

538
00:22:38,730 --> 00:22:43,042
if your ad optimizations can extend the time window,

539
00:22:43,042 --> 00:22:47,440
that would deliver far greater value to advertisers.

540
00:22:47,630 --> 00:22:51,689
But before us, most optimization windows in the market

541
00:22:51,689 --> 00:22:53,068
were just one day,

542
00:22:53,068 --> 00:22:55,290
with rare ones lasting seven

543
00:22:55,650 --> 00:22:58,904
but no one had ever used a rigorous model

544
00:22:58,904 --> 00:23:00,810
beyond a seven-day span

545
00:23:01,010 --> 00:23:04,249
we are the first firm globally to extend it from 7 to 28 days

546
00:23:04,530 --> 00:23:05,797
once our product launched,

547
00:23:05,797 --> 00:23:08,890
it became the hottest item in the entire industry right away.

548
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:11,797
I believe that compared to many other firms,

549
00:23:11,797 --> 00:23:15,062
we truly place the problems our product aims to solve

550
00:23:15,062 --> 00:23:16,850
at the top of our priorities.

551
00:23:17,170 --> 00:23:18,664
then we seek out and ponder

552
00:23:18,664 --> 00:23:21,930
which technological advances could help us reach this goal

553
00:23:22,100 --> 00:23:24,617
rather than picking the most Trending topic first,

554
00:23:24,617 --> 00:23:26,755
then thinking about what tech innovations

555
00:23:26,755 --> 00:23:28,060
we could build around it

556
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:30,805
this is all about LTV—products

557
00:23:30,805 --> 00:23:33,560
and algorithms built around it.

558
00:23:33,720 --> 00:23:35,928
And who came up with this idea?

559
00:23:35,928 --> 00:23:40,059
Or is it tied to the company's core values and its mission?

560
00:23:40,540 --> 00:23:42,386
many might think it doesn't matter

561
00:23:42,386 --> 00:23:43,962
who came up with these ideas,

562
00:23:43,962 --> 00:23:45,948
since anyone working in the industry

563
00:23:45,948 --> 00:23:47,440
knows how crucial they are

564
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:50,980
Then why don't Meta and Google build around this very thing?

565
00:23:50,980 --> 00:23:53,940
Well, I don't know—I'm not Meta's CPO

566
00:23:54,220 --> 00:23:56,420
I think what matters more here

567
00:23:56,420 --> 00:23:59,355
is that once you know this is important,

568
00:23:59,355 --> 00:24:03,390
how you manage and allocate internal company resources

569
00:24:03,670 --> 00:24:09,599
how to effectively help your team tackle this very problem

570
00:24:09,680 --> 00:24:11,497
I think this is the most crucial thing;

571
00:24:11,497 --> 00:24:13,919
I believe everyone in the industry knows it is vital

572
00:24:14,040 --> 00:24:16,880
whether we can solve it — that might be the key issue indeed

573
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:19,985
Let's talk about other competitors—besides Meta and Google,

574
00:24:19,985 --> 00:24:22,739
are there smaller ones that might be potential rivals?

575
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:26,062
I saw folks asking—like Unity, Iron Source,

576
00:24:26,062 --> 00:24:30,413
Char Boost, Lift Off—so why is it AppLovin that's emerging,

577
00:24:30,413 --> 00:24:31,100
not them?

578
00:24:31,380 --> 00:24:34,329
you've been calling these firms 'competitors,'

579
00:24:34,329 --> 00:24:38,240
but to us, we don't see them as actual competitors at all

580
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:41,360
I think these firms are more like partner companies

581
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:43,939
because we ultimately serve folks

582
00:24:43,939 --> 00:24:46,516
like mobile developers and advertisers —

583
00:24:46,516 --> 00:24:47,970
that sort of audience.

584
00:24:48,300 --> 00:24:49,757
especially for mobile developers,

585
00:24:49,757 --> 00:24:51,580
you might wonder why our platform would...

586
00:24:51,940 --> 00:24:55,185
would allow and welcome Google, Meta, Amazon

587
00:24:55,185 --> 00:24:57,040
to bid on our platform,

588
00:24:57,040 --> 00:24:57,890
wouldn't we?

589
00:24:58,090 --> 00:25:00,151
because their purchasing power

590
00:25:00,151 --> 00:25:02,420
actually benefits our developers

591
00:25:02,540 --> 00:25:04,704
so in my view, we are all partners

592
00:25:04,704 --> 00:25:07,460
working together within the same industry

593
00:25:07,740 --> 00:25:10,310
and why have we come out on top in this space?

594
00:25:10,310 --> 00:25:12,785
but in fact there are two distinct industries

595
00:25:12,785 --> 00:25:15,590
here — we are indeed talking about two separate ones

596
00:25:15,980 --> 00:25:19,260
just two distinct branches within one broad industry

597
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:21,965
one is the ad exchange—today

598
00:25:21,965 --> 00:25:26,130
Google and Applovin are the two largest exchanges?

599
00:25:26,510 --> 00:25:28,388
and there are also some smaller ad exchanges,

600
00:25:28,388 --> 00:25:30,310
like Unity's ironSource, which is one as well

601
00:25:30,350 --> 00:25:33,682
but I think the main purpose of this ad exchange platform

602
00:25:33,682 --> 00:25:36,430
is not to generate direct revenue for the firm

603
00:25:36,790 --> 00:25:38,366
its greatest value

604
00:25:38,366 --> 00:25:42,920
lies in supporting the broader mobile app ecosystem

605
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:45,437
gives these developers a platform

606
00:25:45,437 --> 00:25:47,810
where they can monetize their apps?

607
00:25:47,890 --> 00:25:48,617
so for Applovin,

608
00:25:48,617 --> 00:25:50,996
this platform isn't their main source of revenue

609
00:25:50,996 --> 00:25:52,210
or primary income stream

610
00:25:52,370 --> 00:25:53,561
but its or its revenue

611
00:25:53,561 --> 00:25:56,160
makes up just a very tiny fraction of the total.

612
00:25:56,440 --> 00:25:58,760
the other arm of this is the ad-serving segment

613
00:25:59,040 --> 00:26:01,158
once we had such an ecosystem in place,

614
00:26:01,158 --> 00:26:03,705
of course we'd hope that one day we could, say,

615
00:26:03,705 --> 00:26:04,840
run our ads on Meta

616
00:26:04,840 --> 00:26:05,634
run ads on Google,

617
00:26:05,634 --> 00:26:08,160
but I doubt they’ll ever let us do that in the future

618
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:10,203
so we can only place our ads

619
00:26:10,203 --> 00:26:12,848
on that third-party aggregation platform

620
00:26:12,848 --> 00:26:14,359
I just mentioned earlier.

621
00:26:14,460 --> 00:26:16,347
this is why, from my perspective,

622
00:26:16,347 --> 00:26:19,940
sustaining such an ecosystem is truly of utmost importance

623
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:22,144
once we run ads in this space,

624
00:26:22,144 --> 00:26:25,320
you could say we now have competitors to contend with

625
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:28,465
the competitors here aren't just Unity or ironSource,

626
00:26:28,465 --> 00:26:31,169
but all potential bidders like Google and others.

627
00:26:31,170 --> 00:26:31,967
everyone at Meta,

628
00:26:31,967 --> 00:26:32,577
so in my view,

629
00:26:32,577 --> 00:26:35,299
our main competitors are more Google and Meta than others

630
00:26:35,300 --> 00:26:37,397
because looking at our purchase volumes,

631
00:26:37,397 --> 00:26:38,598
their volumes and ours

632
00:26:38,598 --> 00:26:41,000
tend to rise and fall in tandem quite often.

633
00:26:41,160 --> 00:26:43,232
In 2023, your models became more stable,

634
00:26:43,232 --> 00:26:46,400
and you started preparing your Asia-based team as well.

635
00:26:46,480 --> 00:26:46,480
Yes

636
00:26:47,350 --> 00:26:49,736
and you also joined the senior leadership team,

637
00:26:49,736 --> 00:26:50,471
taking on more

638
00:26:50,471 --> 00:26:53,464
business responsibilities—what was the biggest challenge

639
00:26:53,464 --> 00:26:53,990
you faced?

640
00:26:54,070 --> 00:26:58,263
about six months after I joined the company,

641
00:26:58,263 --> 00:27:02,360
which would be around April or May of 2023

642
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:06,780
at that time, we launched the AXON 2.0 model for the first time.

643
00:27:06,780 --> 00:27:10,930
But only part of the models were deployed, not all of them.

644
00:27:11,330 --> 00:27:12,461
but I think that marked

645
00:27:12,461 --> 00:27:14,330
a rather significant milestone indeed

646
00:27:14,530 --> 00:27:17,058
because before that, we weren't quite sure

647
00:27:17,058 --> 00:27:20,019
just how much benefit this approach would yield

648
00:27:20,019 --> 00:27:21,130
once it went live.

649
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:25,354
I recall that before our first model went live,

650
00:27:25,354 --> 00:27:28,291
the team would often prepare a bit

651
00:27:28,291 --> 00:27:30,970
and then run small-scale tests.

652
00:27:31,370 --> 00:27:33,994
at that time our Infrastructure was quite weak,

653
00:27:33,994 --> 00:27:36,506
so whenever we deployed and tested this model,

654
00:27:36,506 --> 00:27:39,020
we'd have to wait roughly an hour in between

655
00:27:39,380 --> 00:27:40,918
then we started joking —

656
00:27:40,918 --> 00:27:42,991
maybe during that one-hour wait,

657
00:27:42,991 --> 00:27:46,670
everyone should buy tickets to Vegas and throw a party

658
00:27:46,710 --> 00:27:50,150
the model went live then, but it wasn't a hit

659
00:27:50,310 --> 00:27:53,190
They said OK, let's delay; we'll head back and iterate

660
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:55,375
we just kept at it like that,

661
00:27:55,375 --> 00:27:57,960
on and off, for about one or two months.

662
00:27:57,960 --> 00:27:59,930
Of course, when the model finally went live,

663
00:27:59,930 --> 00:28:02,510
I felt people had lost that sense of wild, euphoric joy.

664
00:28:02,830 --> 00:28:04,926
but this was surely a milestone for the company —

665
00:28:04,926 --> 00:28:06,310
one I deemed quite significant

666
00:28:07,310 --> 00:28:09,549
Then, after another six months passed,

667
00:28:09,549 --> 00:28:13,119
the following half-year was just constant, nonstop iteration.

668
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:15,103
once the first model went live,

669
00:28:15,103 --> 00:28:18,530
one major issue we faced was quite significant for us

670
00:28:18,950 --> 00:28:22,141
because that first model marked a clear leap —

671
00:28:22,141 --> 00:28:24,390
a generational shift in models

672
00:28:24,510 --> 00:28:27,210
we took AppLovin's model from one that was

673
00:28:27,810 --> 00:28:30,308
a model that lagged the state-of-the-art

674
00:28:30,308 --> 00:28:31,933
by one or two generations

675
00:28:31,933 --> 00:28:34,870
was brought up to a much more advanced version

676
00:28:35,150 --> 00:28:38,711
so the next question is: once the generational gap is closed,

677
00:28:38,711 --> 00:28:41,707
does your model still have the capacity to evolve

678
00:28:41,707 --> 00:28:43,420
and keep generating returns?

679
00:28:43,660 --> 00:28:45,500
Indeed, from the outside at that time,

680
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:48,620
this was one of the biggest doubts people had about AppLovin.

681
00:28:48,960 --> 00:28:50,735
You can see that in 2023,

682
00:28:50,735 --> 00:28:52,278
our earnings reports

683
00:28:52,278 --> 00:28:55,598
consistently far outperformed expectations

684
00:28:55,598 --> 00:28:56,370
each time.

685
00:28:56,730 --> 00:29:00,970
but after the earnings report, our stock price barely budged.

686
00:29:01,310 --> 00:29:04,811
the reason back then was that the industry always thought

687
00:29:04,811 --> 00:29:06,118
our one-time earnings

688
00:29:06,118 --> 00:29:09,480
would not be repeatable or sustainable moving forward.

689
00:29:09,640 --> 00:29:10,632
As for ourselves,

690
00:29:10,632 --> 00:29:11,387
I think—well,

691
00:29:11,387 --> 00:29:14,600
because I've seen how this industry actually works?

692
00:29:14,600 --> 00:29:17,375
I do have some confidence in its iterability,

693
00:29:17,375 --> 00:29:19,710
though it will take time to prove it.

694
00:29:20,430 --> 00:29:22,950
so by the end of 2023 then

695
00:29:23,340 --> 00:29:24,594
during that period,

696
00:29:24,594 --> 00:29:27,591
I believe we proved this model is iterable

697
00:29:27,591 --> 00:29:30,100
and didn't falter once it went live?

698
00:29:30,180 --> 00:29:32,093
Ah, profits are just... uh,

699
00:29:32,093 --> 00:29:35,669
further profits would just stop right there.

700
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:39,205
By early 2024, AppLovin's ambitions had outgrown its $10-billion gaming ads focus.

701
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:48,400
its aim was global digital ads worth trillions of dollars

702
00:29:48,600 --> 00:29:51,840
and the first sector to enter was e-commerce, indeed

703
00:29:52,120 --> 00:29:52,812
In May 2024,

704
00:29:52,812 --> 00:29:56,480
Giovanni Ge formally announced a push into e-commerce

705
00:29:56,640 --> 00:29:59,196
this seemed almost mad in tech circles,

706
00:29:59,196 --> 00:30:02,560
for game ads rely on virtual sense of achievement

707
00:30:02,720 --> 00:30:06,160
and e-commerce ads rely on tangible goods and re-sales

708
00:30:06,280 --> 00:30:10,145
there was widespread doubt about whether AppLovin's ad model

709
00:30:10,145 --> 00:30:12,400
could work in the e-commerce space

710
00:30:12,860 --> 00:30:13,778
By 2024, this year

711
00:30:13,778 --> 00:30:16,780
I feel is when you truly started to shine bright

712
00:30:17,180 --> 00:30:21,578
and gradually, the underdog was embraced by the mainstream,

713
00:30:21,578 --> 00:30:24,510
as your stock prices soared that year.

714
00:30:24,510 --> 00:30:26,235
there were also many doubts raised

715
00:30:26,235 --> 00:30:27,657
this year—what three things

716
00:30:27,657 --> 00:30:29,840
do you think your team got right this year?

717
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:31,291
OK, let me correct you

718
00:30:31,291 --> 00:30:33,539
first—you said we went from 'ana dog'

719
00:30:33,539 --> 00:30:35,351
to being mainstream-accepted,

720
00:30:35,351 --> 00:30:38,979
but I think we've always been a self-recognized 'on the dog'.

721
00:30:39,300 --> 00:30:42,540
and I have always felt proud to carry this identity myself

722
00:30:42,580 --> 00:30:44,220
What is 'self-recognized'

723
00:30:44,460 --> 00:30:47,330
I don't know how the world sees us right now, mind.

724
00:30:47,530 --> 00:30:47,530
Right?

725
00:30:47,890 --> 00:30:48,741
some folks might think

726
00:30:48,741 --> 00:30:50,290
you see yourselves as the underdog here

727
00:30:50,290 --> 00:30:51,718
Yeah, some might think that AppLovin

728
00:30:51,718 --> 00:30:52,983
has done quite well these days,

729
00:30:52,983 --> 00:30:55,129
but others might say that AppLovin is just a company

730
00:30:55,129 --> 00:30:56,740
that got lucky in a short span of time

731
00:30:57,320 --> 00:30:59,410
Hmm, got lucky briefly, hyped up

732
00:30:59,410 --> 00:31:01,920
one viral hit company after another?

733
00:31:02,130 --> 00:31:04,170
but I think none of these matter much to us at all

734
00:31:04,250 --> 00:31:06,930
we see ourselves as the underdog in this race

735
00:31:06,970 --> 00:31:07,841
I think this is,

736
00:31:07,841 --> 00:31:09,552
and we use the 'underdog' ethos

737
00:31:09,552 --> 00:31:11,501
to shape our cultural foundation

738
00:31:11,501 --> 00:31:13,360
and many of our working methods.

739
00:31:13,780 --> 00:31:15,740
this, to me, is the most important

740
00:31:15,780 --> 00:31:18,820
You translate 'underdog' as 'Heart of the Weak' in Chinese?

741
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:20,728
Could you explain once more

742
00:31:20,728 --> 00:31:23,260
what your understanding of this term is?

743
00:31:23,260 --> 00:31:26,510
I think a better, perhaps more accurate translation

744
00:31:26,510 --> 00:31:27,940
would be 'the underdog,'

745
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:31,580
while 'weak heart' was just to fit a four-character idiom

746
00:31:31,770 --> 00:31:33,215
I think 'underdog'

747
00:31:33,215 --> 00:31:37,210
carries quite a few distinct layers of meaning,

748
00:31:37,210 --> 00:31:38,570
wouldn't you say?

749
00:31:38,850 --> 00:31:40,710
First, I think 'underdog'

750
00:31:40,710 --> 00:31:43,784
means that you won't, won't be arrogant

751
00:31:43,784 --> 00:31:45,160
or overconfident?

752
00:31:45,160 --> 00:31:48,140
you lack the right to be conceited, for others

753
00:31:48,140 --> 00:31:49,200
see you as weak

754
00:31:49,840 --> 00:31:52,788
and secondly, I think as an underdog,

755
00:31:52,788 --> 00:31:57,760
you don't carry the weight of fear of failing when you act.

756
00:31:58,200 --> 00:32:00,536
because you're the underdog anyway—yes,

757
00:32:00,536 --> 00:32:02,320
no one thinks you'll succeed?

758
00:32:02,440 --> 00:32:05,330
the opposite case — someone who has known nothing but success,

759
00:32:05,330 --> 00:32:06,800
a true prodigy all their life

760
00:32:07,120 --> 00:32:09,961
you might be less likely to make a choice

761
00:32:09,961 --> 00:32:12,110
that carries huge risks indeed

762
00:32:12,110 --> 00:32:13,854
because you've been so successful,

763
00:32:13,854 --> 00:32:16,691
you don't want to break that image—unlike an underdog,

764
00:32:16,691 --> 00:32:18,190
who doesn't face this issue.

765
00:32:18,690 --> 00:32:23,010
and I think underdogs tend to have a much lower sense of ego

766
00:32:23,230 --> 00:32:26,369
in one problem after problem, in one city or one mission,

767
00:32:26,369 --> 00:32:29,110
he does not see himself as being very important.

768
00:32:29,470 --> 00:32:31,558
he would value the mission and the team

769
00:32:31,558 --> 00:32:32,790
above his own concerns

770
00:32:33,270 --> 00:32:35,254
I think also, many underdogs

771
00:32:35,254 --> 00:32:38,488
have this strong desire to prove themselves,

772
00:32:38,488 --> 00:32:41,870
since most people don't expect much from them?

773
00:32:42,110 --> 00:32:43,858
and this desire to prove oneself,

774
00:32:43,858 --> 00:32:46,410
well, it can generate a massive amount of drive

775
00:32:46,410 --> 00:32:47,465
for an individual.

776
00:32:47,465 --> 00:32:49,760
You mentioned the three right things to do?

777
00:32:50,140 --> 00:32:52,997
I don't think this is how we approach things

778
00:32:52,997 --> 00:32:55,660
here at AppLovin in our thinking process

779
00:32:55,980 --> 00:33:00,747
I would never reduce 365 days of hard work

780
00:33:00,747 --> 00:33:07,540
to just three correct decisions in such a simplistic way?

781
00:33:07,660 --> 00:33:11,382
I think such a claim would actually harm the entire team

782
00:33:11,382 --> 00:33:12,180
quite a bit

783
00:33:12,290 --> 00:33:14,743
because there are dozens of people on the team,

784
00:33:14,743 --> 00:33:16,343
each doing small bits of work,

785
00:33:16,343 --> 00:33:19,010
and those small bits of work come together as one

786
00:33:19,490 --> 00:33:21,170
led to our success

787
00:33:21,410 --> 00:33:22,089
I don't think

788
00:33:22,089 --> 00:33:24,653
there are three things you can isolate and claim

789
00:33:24,653 --> 00:33:27,270
those three things alone made AppLovin successful

790
00:33:27,350 --> 00:33:29,910
No, I don't think that is what actually happened

791
00:33:30,330 --> 00:33:30,338
Okay, no problem. Hmm, so this year, many institutional research reports had headlines stating AppLovin is an 'AI beneficiary stock,' right?

792
00:33:39,080 --> 00:33:40,103
and how does your team

793
00:33:40,103 --> 00:33:42,081
inside view this matter—does AI truly play

794
00:33:42,081 --> 00:33:43,024
such a crucial role

795
00:33:43,024 --> 00:33:43,920
in your algorithms?

796
00:33:44,440 --> 00:33:48,722
First, on AI — I think the definition of AI itself

797
00:33:48,722 --> 00:33:50,910
is quite vague nowadays

798
00:33:50,910 --> 00:33:50,910
Yes

799
00:33:51,990 --> 00:33:53,515
in today's mainstream,

800
00:33:53,515 --> 00:33:56,348
and what I see as the common narrative,

801
00:33:56,348 --> 00:33:58,335
AI is largely taken to mean

802
00:33:58,335 --> 00:34:01,870
LLMs — Large Language Models — and related concepts.

803
00:34:01,870 --> 00:34:02,230
for Large LLMs

804
00:34:02,630 --> 00:34:03,885
but the term 'AI' itself

805
00:34:03,885 --> 00:34:06,910
was often mentioned long before LLMs came into being.

806
00:34:07,110 --> 00:34:09,982
In fact, the recommendation algorithm

807
00:34:09,982 --> 00:34:13,094
was the most successful AI application

808
00:34:13,094 --> 00:34:14,690
before LLMs emerged?

809
00:34:14,850 --> 00:34:17,157
so Applion, as a top-tier firm

810
00:34:17,157 --> 00:34:20,179
in the recommendation algorithm space,

811
00:34:20,179 --> 00:34:22,925
surely employs all AI technologies

812
00:34:22,925 --> 00:34:25,670
tied to recommendation algorithms.

813
00:34:26,170 --> 00:34:28,782
And regarding large language models, right?

814
00:34:28,782 --> 00:34:29,688
It's no secret

815
00:34:29,688 --> 00:34:32,860
that AppLovin is not training its own LLMs today.

816
00:34:33,139 --> 00:34:36,100
but we do indeed use large language models

817
00:34:36,100 --> 00:34:37,510
across our products

818
00:34:37,510 --> 00:34:41,460
to enhance and refine both our offerings and algorithms

819
00:34:41,860 --> 00:34:44,371
This includes our products and algorithms —

820
00:34:44,371 --> 00:34:46,583
we do use LLMs within our algorithms.

821
00:34:46,583 --> 00:34:47,898
That is, indeed, a given.

822
00:34:48,260 --> 00:34:52,112
And, uh, as for what the outside world thinks—do we say,

823
00:34:52,112 --> 00:34:53,929
uh, is EbolaMi an AI firm,

824
00:34:53,929 --> 00:34:55,530
or does Iepilog exist?

825
00:34:55,770 --> 00:34:56,650
Uh, because,

826
00:34:56,920 --> 00:34:59,320
Hmm, AI benefits — well, inside the company,

827
00:34:59,320 --> 00:35:01,300
we never think about this at all,

828
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:04,720
nor do we care what others might say or how they judge us.

829
00:35:04,720 --> 00:35:07,213
In our view, AppLovin is not an AI firm,

830
00:35:07,213 --> 00:35:10,560
and it has no bearing on our daily decision-making.

831
00:35:10,600 --> 00:35:13,924
You just said AI would be involved in every stage —

832
00:35:13,924 --> 00:35:16,450
when you mention AI here, you mean LLMs,

833
00:35:16,450 --> 00:35:18,910
which empower decisions at each step

834
00:35:19,230 --> 00:35:20,870
such an approach then

835
00:35:21,470 --> 00:35:22,330
Hmm, methods.

836
00:35:22,330 --> 00:35:25,290
In this industry, I believe other firms would do the same

837
00:35:25,290 --> 00:35:25,290
Yes

838
00:35:25,850 --> 00:35:26,730
Meta has too

839
00:35:26,730 --> 00:35:27,797
so why did we see it —

840
00:35:27,797 --> 00:35:30,034
its stock price surged quite a bit back then,

841
00:35:30,034 --> 00:35:32,729
and they claimed their ads had become more effective

842
00:35:32,850 --> 00:35:35,470
Are there big gaps? Like, how do we use AI,

843
00:35:35,470 --> 00:35:37,370
and how does AI boost models?

844
00:35:37,500 --> 00:35:39,187
Do each firm differ this much?

845
00:35:39,187 --> 00:35:41,099
Then, where lies your key advantage?

846
00:35:41,180 --> 00:35:43,728
Oh, I think in this current, uh, scenario,

847
00:35:43,728 --> 00:35:47,253
one major strength we have is that our company isn't

848
00:35:47,253 --> 00:35:49,830
building our own large language model .

849
00:35:50,170 --> 00:35:52,510
since we don't build our own LLM,

850
00:35:52,510 --> 00:35:54,587
we have full freedom to pick

851
00:35:54,587 --> 00:35:57,629
the best models available on the market —

852
00:35:57,629 --> 00:35:58,799
isn't that right?

853
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:01,647
although many firms are building their own,

854
00:36:01,647 --> 00:36:03,492
I think most would agree that

855
00:36:03,492 --> 00:36:06,738
the best large language models available today are

856
00:36:06,738 --> 00:36:08,330
Gemini and OpenAI's LLMs

857
00:36:08,530 --> 00:36:10,770
and Gemini, plus OpenAI models?

858
00:36:11,050 --> 00:36:12,846
since we didn't build our own,

859
00:36:12,846 --> 00:36:14,228
we gained more freedom

860
00:36:14,228 --> 00:36:17,120
to pick the top language models on the market.

861
00:36:17,390 --> 00:36:17,398
Hmm, and that's why I think in terms of LLM adoption and application, we've actually led the world in many ways.

862
00:36:25,720 --> 00:36:28,522
for instance, more than a year ago,

863
00:36:28,522 --> 00:36:33,280
we successfully used LLMs to tackle challenges we faced

864
00:36:33,760 --> 00:36:36,898
we faced some Fraud and Integrity issues

865
00:36:36,898 --> 00:36:39,880
in the ad delivery and campaign space

866
00:36:40,080 --> 00:36:41,839
and, well, in recent months,

867
00:36:41,839 --> 00:36:44,183
we became the first in the market

868
00:36:44,183 --> 00:36:47,423
to launch fully automated ad asset generation

869
00:36:47,423 --> 00:36:49,440
using Large Language Models .

870
00:36:49,840 --> 00:36:51,072
this, this right here —

871
00:36:51,072 --> 00:36:53,478
yes — we've already rolled out this product

872
00:36:53,478 --> 00:36:54,652
to our advertisers;

873
00:36:54,652 --> 00:36:56,999
we're leading the industry in this space.

874
00:36:57,200 --> 00:37:00,018
and the reason we managed to achieve this

875
00:37:00,018 --> 00:37:01,393
ahead of our rivals

876
00:37:01,393 --> 00:37:03,975
is because I made a decision early on

877
00:37:03,975 --> 00:37:05,930
that I believed strongly in

878
00:37:06,050 --> 00:37:09,250
for instance, in ad content generation here,

879
00:37:09,970 --> 00:37:12,302
I believe that in the short term,

880
00:37:12,302 --> 00:37:16,530
only two firms excel: Google and OpenAI stand out the most.

881
00:37:16,690 --> 00:37:18,750
we don't need to do in this space

882
00:37:18,750 --> 00:37:21,560
what they've already done exceptionally well?

883
00:37:21,760 --> 00:37:25,585
and we, in turn, focused on how to use their models

884
00:37:25,585 --> 00:37:29,490
and integrate them into our own product offerings.

885
00:37:29,760 --> 00:37:32,633
and this decision — you should know it was made one year ago,

886
00:37:32,633 --> 00:37:35,160
when Google and OpenAI's models were not yet ready

887
00:37:35,440 --> 00:37:36,612
one of our choices back then

888
00:37:36,612 --> 00:37:38,080
was to trust our partner companies

889
00:37:38,330 --> 00:37:38,330
Right?

890
00:37:38,530 --> 00:37:40,507
we at Google, Magoogle, and Open I — to us,

891
00:37:40,507 --> 00:37:41,622
they are our partners.

892
00:37:41,622 --> 00:37:43,092
Because we trust our partners,

893
00:37:43,092 --> 00:37:45,729
we do things they wouldn't necessarily do ourselves.

894
00:37:45,930 --> 00:37:47,519
we waited on the other end of time,

895
00:37:47,519 --> 00:37:49,810
knowing they'd build this model one day for sure.

896
00:37:50,050 --> 00:37:52,085
so when Veo 3 and Sora 2 were released,

897
00:37:52,085 --> 00:37:54,660
that was the moment — The Right Moment comes.

898
00:37:55,180 --> 00:37:57,359
so by then our entire product flow was fully ready,

899
00:37:57,359 --> 00:37:58,710
just waiting for their models —

900
00:37:58,710 --> 00:38:01,019
once theirs were done, our product launched right away.

901
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:02,150
so it still feels

902
00:38:02,150 --> 00:38:04,917
like a race for speed—whomever can use this tool best

903
00:38:04,917 --> 00:38:06,608
and bring it to state-of-the-art,

904
00:38:06,608 --> 00:38:08,760
that is the cutting-edge level we aim for

905
00:38:08,820 --> 00:38:08,820
Yes.

906
00:38:09,140 --> 00:38:09,860
launch the product

907
00:38:09,860 --> 00:38:11,774
Yes, I think it's not just speed,

908
00:38:11,774 --> 00:38:14,740
but also an art of making choices and decisions

909
00:38:15,140 --> 00:38:18,129
there are many things—even the most trending ones,

910
00:38:18,129 --> 00:38:20,101
even the most exciting ones—that

911
00:38:20,101 --> 00:38:22,135
choosing not to do them yourself

912
00:38:22,135 --> 00:38:24,170
can sometimes be a wise decision

913
00:38:24,250 --> 00:38:25,702
But for some reason, I feel

914
00:38:25,702 --> 00:38:29,000
many people in this world struggle to accept such a choice.

915
00:38:29,280 --> 00:38:31,537
I always feel that if something is the most exciting,

916
00:38:31,537 --> 00:38:32,840
I must choose to do it myself.

917
00:38:32,880 --> 00:38:35,311
Let's imagine Google in the future

918
00:38:35,311 --> 00:38:37,600
might decide to cut off its API

919
00:38:37,680 --> 00:38:40,751
then these model firms might want to run ads themselves,

920
00:38:40,751 --> 00:38:43,067
and Peng Da would want to run ads himself,

921
00:38:43,067 --> 00:38:45,610
and might cut off access to this Model A API.

922
00:38:45,850 --> 00:38:46,890
I don't think this will.

923
00:38:47,250 --> 00:38:49,479
I think if someone truly believed that,

924
00:38:49,479 --> 00:38:51,650
their decision might be rather naive.

925
00:38:52,330 --> 00:38:52,330
Yes.

926
00:38:53,250 --> 00:38:57,553
Today, we are still one of Google's largest clients, right?

927
00:38:57,553 --> 00:39:01,479
Because we are a major user of Google Cloud services.

928
00:39:01,800 --> 00:39:05,334
if we are worried that Google might cut off our access,

929
00:39:05,334 --> 00:39:08,725
shouldn't we also worry about not being able to use

930
00:39:08,725 --> 00:39:09,590
Google Cloud?

931
00:39:10,230 --> 00:39:13,847
In 2024, one of the biggest things—and what outsiders

932
00:39:13,847 --> 00:39:15,729
are most curious about—is

933
00:39:15,729 --> 00:39:18,190
that you've moved into e-commerce .

934
00:39:18,190 --> 00:39:19,672
Yes, could you share with us

935
00:39:19,672 --> 00:39:21,539
the reasoning and thought process

936
00:39:21,539 --> 00:39:24,120
behind your decision to expand into e-commerce?

937
00:39:24,200 --> 00:39:27,009
Oh, this line of thinking is actually quite simple.

938
00:39:27,009 --> 00:39:29,360
When we chat, I notice you're quite eager.

939
00:39:30,110 --> 00:39:34,350
Was our success due to a single, specific decision?

940
00:39:34,350 --> 00:39:35,841
But that's not quite right —

941
00:39:35,841 --> 00:39:37,553
the decision itself was simple.

942
00:39:37,553 --> 00:39:39,596
What truly led the company to success

943
00:39:39,596 --> 00:39:42,579
was how we lived each of the 365 days after that choice.

944
00:39:42,820 --> 00:39:44,785
So when we look back to early 2024,

945
00:39:44,785 --> 00:39:46,334
there were several factors

946
00:39:46,334 --> 00:39:48,956
I think were quite significant at the time —

947
00:39:48,956 --> 00:39:51,339
first, our tech in mobile app advertising

948
00:39:51,810 --> 00:39:53,038
in mobile app ads,

949
00:39:53,038 --> 00:39:54,355
we've proven we're

950
00:39:54,355 --> 00:39:57,795
at least on par with top-tier global companies

951
00:39:57,795 --> 00:39:58,820
in this space

952
00:39:58,940 --> 00:40:01,540
we are fully capable of competing with them head-on

953
00:40:01,880 --> 00:40:04,194
Second, the mobile app sector we're in

954
00:40:04,194 --> 00:40:06,760
doesn't have a particularly high ceiling.

955
00:40:07,200 --> 00:40:08,870
so the market feels that

956
00:40:08,870 --> 00:40:12,143
even if AppLovin excels greatly in mobile apps,

957
00:40:12,143 --> 00:40:14,580
our ceiling isn't that high either

958
00:40:15,260 --> 00:40:18,965
thirdly, I have a clear view on e-commerce

959
00:40:18,965 --> 00:40:22,400
and what algorithms can achieve there

960
00:40:22,560 --> 00:40:25,520
I have a fairly solid grasp of its feasibility

961
00:40:25,560 --> 00:40:28,172
I believe our tech at that time

962
00:40:28,172 --> 00:40:31,290
could be fully applied to e-commerce

963
00:40:31,420 --> 00:40:32,533
so given this context,

964
00:40:32,533 --> 00:40:34,654
we had a very brief internal discussion

965
00:40:34,654 --> 00:40:36,429
and didn't spend much time before

966
00:40:36,429 --> 00:40:38,260
we decided to pursue this project

967
00:40:38,620 --> 00:40:42,790
This happened roughly around May of the year 2024.

968
00:40:42,990 --> 00:40:46,503
when you venture into a different industry,

969
00:40:46,503 --> 00:40:48,344
or a new market track,

970
00:40:48,344 --> 00:40:52,360
what is it called when the model is transferred?

971
00:40:54,910 --> 00:40:57,388
Hardness is a highly subjective notion.

972
00:40:57,388 --> 00:40:59,549
I believe this is what one must do.

973
00:40:59,710 --> 00:41:00,910
what needs to be done.

974
00:41:00,910 --> 00:41:03,344
I think first, this should be feasible,

975
00:41:03,344 --> 00:41:04,369
wouldn't you say?

976
00:41:04,650 --> 00:41:09,160
I think you need a team that excels at solving problems

977
00:41:09,160 --> 00:41:12,030
and thinking via 'First Principles'.

978
00:41:12,670 --> 00:41:15,902
Yes, at this point when you move—from a domain

979
00:41:15,902 --> 00:41:17,870
you're familiar with—to one

980
00:41:17,870 --> 00:41:20,859
you're not, or not quite as familiar with.

981
00:41:21,090 --> 00:41:24,650
the most dangerous mistake is blindly applying past wisdom

982
00:41:25,230 --> 00:41:27,209
at this point, it was indeed necessary —

983
00:41:27,209 --> 00:41:30,229
I recall the small decisions our whole team made back then.

984
00:41:30,230 --> 00:41:32,758
I think First Principles thinking was especially crucial

985
00:41:32,758 --> 00:41:33,210
back then.

986
00:41:33,290 --> 00:41:35,050
For instance, could you share some examples with us?

987
00:41:35,050 --> 00:41:35,770
For instance,

988
00:41:35,930 --> 00:41:40,194
Yes, for instance, how does it work in gaming,

989
00:41:40,194 --> 00:41:45,650
and what are the challenges when applied to e-commerce?

990
00:41:45,650 --> 00:41:48,769
for instance, its data—I don't know—what sorts of challenges

991
00:41:48,769 --> 00:41:49,404
might arise

992
00:41:49,404 --> 00:41:51,096
with the data or the algorithms,

993
00:41:51,096 --> 00:41:53,000
and how did you tackle those issues

994
00:41:53,040 --> 00:41:56,211
OK, first you need to realize it's a different product —

995
00:41:56,211 --> 00:41:58,701
and that means every part of this product

996
00:41:58,701 --> 00:42:00,160
has changed in some way.

997
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:02,197
the advertisers are different,

998
00:42:02,197 --> 00:42:04,511
their data-sharing methods have changed,

999
00:42:04,511 --> 00:42:07,520
and the ad creatives used are now different as well

1000
00:42:07,640 --> 00:42:10,241
once the ad is clicked, the landing page changes,

1001
00:42:10,241 --> 00:42:12,400
and indeed, everything is different now

1002
00:42:12,640 --> 00:42:14,160
So at this point

1003
00:42:14,600 --> 00:42:17,173
the value of First Principles lies in discerning

1004
00:42:17,173 --> 00:42:19,587
which differences can be directly translated,

1005
00:42:19,587 --> 00:42:21,840
and which require new approaches from you.

1006
00:42:22,060 --> 00:42:24,020
you need different handling methods

1007
00:42:24,020 --> 00:42:25,700
and changes within your model?

1008
00:42:26,030 --> 00:42:29,076
once you've deployed your first, fairly simple model

1009
00:42:29,076 --> 00:42:30,630
and a basic system online

1010
00:42:30,710 --> 00:42:33,138
it will surely fall short of expectations,

1011
00:42:33,138 --> 00:42:34,679
since you're a new system?

1012
00:42:34,880 --> 00:42:36,626
when it falls short of expectations,

1013
00:42:36,626 --> 00:42:38,573
you should know which part to examine —

1014
00:42:38,573 --> 00:42:40,520
you might think this part is quite good

1015
00:42:40,680 --> 00:42:42,883
you can then figure out what went wrong in that part

1016
00:42:42,883 --> 00:42:44,240
and trace the root of the issue

1017
00:42:44,360 --> 00:42:45,440
was it a data issue,

1018
00:42:45,440 --> 00:42:48,680
an attribution flaw, a model problem, or a feature problem?

1019
00:42:48,880 --> 00:42:51,595
so in this case, you need quite a bit of — well,

1020
00:42:51,595 --> 00:42:55,360
I think very solid and extremely rigorous thinking to assess.

1021
00:42:55,520 --> 00:42:56,898
and back then, when we did this,

1022
00:42:56,898 --> 00:42:58,920
our team was extremely small and quite lean

1023
00:42:59,120 --> 00:43:01,411
for the first phase of e-commerce,

1024
00:43:01,411 --> 00:43:04,050
we had roughly ten engineers on board

1025
00:43:04,090 --> 00:43:06,272
I feel this explanation is still a bit vague;

1026
00:43:06,272 --> 00:43:08,649
perhaps the audience still finds it hard to grasp

1027
00:43:08,810 --> 00:43:11,411
for instance, shifting from games to e-commerce —

1028
00:43:11,411 --> 00:43:13,335
though they’re distinct products,

1029
00:43:13,335 --> 00:43:14,919
what were the key challenges?

1030
00:43:14,920 --> 00:43:16,360
and what problems did you then resolve

1031
00:43:16,600 --> 00:43:17,904
I think you could treat it

1032
00:43:17,904 --> 00:43:19,760
as building a new model from scratch.

1033
00:43:20,330 --> 00:43:23,905
when you have a team that just built the world's

1034
00:43:23,905 --> 00:43:25,097
most successful

1035
00:43:25,097 --> 00:43:26,960
recommendation algorithm

1036
00:43:26,960 --> 00:43:28,600
model for mobile apps?

1037
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:32,983
then much of your infrastructure can be reused —

1038
00:43:32,983 --> 00:43:37,862
for instance, the infrastructure I used to train my models

1039
00:43:37,862 --> 00:43:39,490
can also be reused

1040
00:43:39,850 --> 00:43:43,220
but how do you gather your backend data

1041
00:43:43,260 --> 00:43:45,660
what architecture does your model use

1042
00:43:46,030 --> 00:43:49,196
and what features the model uses may differ greatly,

1043
00:43:49,196 --> 00:43:51,845
since e-commerce features and app features

1044
00:43:51,845 --> 00:43:53,170
can vary quite a lot.

1045
00:43:53,610 --> 00:43:56,223
Yes, so you could see it as almost a fresh startup,

1046
00:43:56,223 --> 00:43:57,930
starting from scratch once more.

1047
00:43:58,250 --> 00:43:59,730
the only thing we had,

1048
00:43:59,730 --> 00:44:02,690
and the only thing that stood back in 2024

1049
00:44:02,930 --> 00:44:04,617
what we have gained compared to 2023

1050
00:44:04,617 --> 00:44:06,690
is a full year of trial-and-error learnings

1051
00:44:08,390 --> 00:44:10,091
Yes. So I think the challenge you just mentioned

1052
00:44:10,091 --> 00:44:11,830
is grasping the abstract nature of this problem

1053
00:44:12,000 --> 00:44:14,121
I think the challenge is that you assume

1054
00:44:14,121 --> 00:44:16,297
there are many things you can copy-paste,

1055
00:44:16,297 --> 00:44:17,200
but there aren't

1056
00:44:17,200 --> 00:44:20,308
you assume that this—everything is almost starting fresh,

1057
00:44:20,308 --> 00:44:21,323
and the only extra

1058
00:44:21,323 --> 00:44:23,749
is the past year’s experience in your mind

1059
00:44:24,110 --> 00:44:25,803
So would it be fair to say that

1060
00:44:25,803 --> 00:44:28,207
since e-commerce is something you can treat

1061
00:44:28,207 --> 00:44:30,448
as a fresh startup to build from scratch,

1062
00:44:30,448 --> 00:44:33,180
you could do the same in other industries as well?

1063
00:44:33,710 --> 00:44:36,100
that wouldn't be just copying this model

1064
00:44:36,140 --> 00:44:37,967
meaning you'll launch new products,

1065
00:44:37,967 --> 00:44:38,895
and in the future,

1066
00:44:38,895 --> 00:44:41,300
you'll target different sectors and markets.

1067
00:44:41,380 --> 00:44:42,380
You are quite right here

1068
00:44:42,620 --> 00:44:45,506
I've never thought AppLovin's strength lies in excelling

1069
00:44:45,506 --> 00:44:47,156
at solving one specific problem —

1070
00:44:47,156 --> 00:44:49,940
which is exactly what I've urged the team to focus on.

1071
00:44:50,260 --> 00:44:52,940
I’ve told the team time and again that,

1072
00:44:53,280 --> 00:44:55,610
Apple-loving our current approach

1073
00:44:55,610 --> 00:44:56,898
gives us the power

1074
00:44:56,898 --> 00:45:01,120
to tackle any meaningful challenge this world has to offer?

1075
00:45:01,320 --> 00:45:03,420
for instance, today AppLovin is not doing that —

1076
00:45:03,420 --> 00:45:06,159
as I said, AppLovin is not training large language models now

1077
00:45:06,380 --> 00:45:08,831
In my view, the best ROI for us right now

1078
00:45:08,831 --> 00:45:11,100
isn't training large language models .

1079
00:45:11,300 --> 00:45:14,978
but if one day we decide we must pursue such a path,

1080
00:45:14,978 --> 00:45:17,467
we could swiftly become a top-tier,

1081
00:45:17,467 --> 00:45:19,810
Tier-1 player on the global stage.

1082
00:45:20,050 --> 00:45:21,409
Yes, I think the outside world

1083
00:45:21,409 --> 00:45:23,050
still misperceives you quite a lot.

1084
00:45:23,050 --> 00:45:23,050
Misread.

1085
00:45:23,410 --> 00:45:25,980
because I read many reports and research notes

1086
00:45:26,100 --> 00:45:27,268
outsiders think that you've

1087
00:45:27,268 --> 00:45:28,740
been making games for so long now

1088
00:45:29,270 --> 00:45:31,555
you had amassed many game assets before,

1089
00:45:31,555 --> 00:45:34,780
so your game-related data gave you an edge over others

1090
00:45:35,060 --> 00:45:37,440
so your models could function effectively

1091
00:45:37,440 --> 00:45:38,660
in the gaming sector

1092
00:45:39,020 --> 00:45:42,032
so when you shifted from games to e-commerce,

1093
00:45:42,032 --> 00:45:44,702
the outside world was quite skeptical,

1094
00:45:44,702 --> 00:45:46,620
yet your e-commerce took off

1095
00:45:46,900 --> 00:45:46,906
Hmm, so folks might think that this is a model transfer, and what exactly did you do during the migration process?

1096
00:45:53,940 --> 00:45:57,000
Yes, folks out there always want some reason given

1097
00:45:57,200 --> 00:45:57,200
Yes.

1098
00:45:57,760 --> 00:46:01,927
make our success seem simple and routine.

1099
00:46:01,927 --> 00:46:07,009
I've grown used to such doubts and underestimation.

1100
00:46:07,290 --> 00:46:11,050
and it hasn't made me feel the least bit uneasy

1101
00:46:11,530 --> 00:46:12,465
but in Bao's sector,

1102
00:46:12,465 --> 00:46:15,024
if one day the outside world changed its view of us,

1103
00:46:15,024 --> 00:46:16,967
and we tried to enter a brand-new field,

1104
00:46:16,967 --> 00:46:19,209
the world might say of AppLovin this company

1105
00:46:19,590 --> 00:46:21,621
they are all very skilled and smart;

1106
00:46:21,621 --> 00:46:23,708
they can succeed at anything they try,

1107
00:46:23,708 --> 00:46:25,909
and they will surely pull this off too

1108
00:46:26,060 --> 00:46:27,636
if that day ever truly comes,

1109
00:46:27,636 --> 00:46:30,620
I think I might feel uneasy and out of place instead

1110
00:46:30,820 --> 00:46:33,180
This is what I mean—this underdog spirit

1111
00:46:33,180 --> 00:46:35,079
we excel at, turning things around

1112
00:46:35,079 --> 00:46:37,439
when no one believed in us to begin with.

1113
00:46:37,760 --> 00:46:39,236
So, do you think your team's strength

1114
00:46:39,236 --> 00:46:40,960
is precisely the fact that it's your team?

1115
00:46:40,960 --> 00:46:40,960
Yes

1116
00:46:41,600 --> 00:46:44,480
This point is quite contrary to what folks think.

1117
00:46:44,600 --> 00:46:46,160
Then let me ask you instead

1118
00:46:46,160 --> 00:46:49,729
So, you're running your own content channel now, right?

1119
00:46:49,729 --> 00:46:53,568
And before that, you worked at a pretty big media platform,

1120
00:46:53,568 --> 00:46:54,309
didn't you?

1121
00:46:54,470 --> 00:46:56,551
when you left to start your own business,

1122
00:46:56,551 --> 00:46:57,760
did everyone around you

1123
00:46:57,760 --> 00:47:00,104
cheer and shout, 'That's brilliant—this choice

1124
00:47:00,104 --> 00:47:01,650
will surely lead to success'?

1125
00:47:01,650 --> 00:47:04,210
No, my mom asks me every day why I chose to go it alone

1126
00:47:04,340 --> 00:47:04,347
Is that right? Yes, do you feel that such doubts, in your life, have in many ways actually served as a source of motivation for you?

1127
00:47:12,700 --> 00:47:14,300
Hmm, that doesn't matter to me.

1128
00:47:14,500 --> 00:47:15,740
Not important

1129
00:47:16,020 --> 00:47:16,028
Okay, let's get back to your story. Right. Hmm, this year, you sold off your gaming business. I should probably ask—why did you decide to sell it?

1130
00:47:24,490 --> 00:47:25,200
OK, I think, uh,

1131
00:47:25,200 --> 00:47:26,364
I can only guess here —

1132
00:47:26,364 --> 00:47:28,970
I wasn't directly involved in this role at all.

1133
00:47:29,290 --> 00:47:31,378
Once the events unfolded,

1134
00:47:31,378 --> 00:47:33,763
Adam told me, 'We're thinking

1135
00:47:33,763 --> 00:47:37,120
of selling the game—what do you think?'

1136
00:47:37,120 --> 00:47:39,209
I said, 'That makes sense.'

1137
00:47:39,640 --> 00:47:43,103
Right? Of course, Adam was the key player and decision-maker,

1138
00:47:43,103 --> 00:47:46,090
and he drove the entire process to make it happen.

1139
00:47:46,170 --> 00:47:49,970
From my own perspective, I think it makes sense too

1140
00:47:49,970 --> 00:47:56,800
before AXON 2.0 was a success

1141
00:47:56,960 --> 00:48:00,280
the company had many different business lines back then.

1142
00:48:00,360 --> 00:48:04,085
but once our recommendation algorithm started working well

1143
00:48:04,085 --> 00:48:06,590
and the e-commerce future became clear

1144
00:48:07,070 --> 00:48:08,552
Hmm, and you can see that,

1145
00:48:08,552 --> 00:48:12,196
well, the ad business—its profit margins are extremely high,

1146
00:48:12,196 --> 00:48:15,100
and the talent concentration is also very high.

1147
00:48:15,380 --> 00:48:16,887
so in this context,

1148
00:48:16,887 --> 00:48:19,693
I think exiting the gaming sector

1149
00:48:19,693 --> 00:48:22,330
was a perfectly sound decision?

1150
00:48:22,690 --> 00:48:25,750
for games, of course, we mentioned that the game data —

1151
00:48:25,750 --> 00:48:26,786
these data points

1152
00:48:26,786 --> 00:48:29,470
aren't that crucial for our core algorithms

1153
00:48:29,670 --> 00:48:31,709
so I think this is a perfectly sound decision,

1154
00:48:31,709 --> 00:48:33,840
even though I did not make the decision myself

1155
00:48:33,920 --> 00:48:35,798
Yes, did folks inside push back back then?

1156
00:48:35,798 --> 00:48:37,160
Were there any doubts raised?

1157
00:48:37,320 --> 00:48:38,210
Hmm, nope, none.

1158
00:48:38,610 --> 00:48:38,610
Nope

1159
00:48:38,930 --> 00:48:41,760
Everyone thinks we'll just go all-in now,

1160
00:48:41,760 --> 00:48:42,440
I think this is AppLovin as well

1161
00:48:42,480 --> 00:48:44,805
Hmm, that's one interesting aspect of Apple Lion.

1162
00:48:44,805 --> 00:48:47,007
I'm not sure if I mentioned this before—that

1163
00:48:47,007 --> 00:48:49,160
we don't have many meetings at the company.

1164
00:48:49,710 --> 00:48:53,396
one reason we rarely hold meetings

1165
00:48:53,396 --> 00:48:59,360
is that everyone is extremely aligned on the direction

1166
00:48:59,800 --> 00:49:02,790
just one matter—even if folks don't talk directly,

1167
00:49:02,790 --> 00:49:04,133
just ask and confirm:

1168
00:49:04,133 --> 00:49:07,429
what do you think is the broad direction of this thing?

1169
00:49:07,430 --> 00:49:10,403
you'll find that everyone shares a remarkably aligned view

1170
00:49:10,403 --> 00:49:11,480
on the big direction

1171
00:49:11,960 --> 00:49:13,488
with this as a starting point,

1172
00:49:13,488 --> 00:49:16,440
you'd find that when everyone agrees on the big picture,

1173
00:49:16,720 --> 00:49:18,991
when each person decides the next steps

1174
00:49:18,991 --> 00:49:21,146
based on their most logical judgment,

1175
00:49:21,146 --> 00:49:22,624
you'll find that everyone

1176
00:49:22,624 --> 00:49:24,990
ends up quite similar in their approach

1177
00:49:25,470 --> 00:49:28,368
so we don't need to spend hours in meetings

1178
00:49:28,368 --> 00:49:30,390
hashing out every tiny detail.

1179
00:49:30,670 --> 00:49:34,226
once everyone gives a brief line on what we should do next,

1180
00:49:34,226 --> 00:49:37,170
then each person can rely on their common sense

1181
00:49:37,570 --> 00:49:40,354
you'll find that the common sense each person uses

1182
00:49:40,354 --> 00:49:42,360
leads to remarkably similar details

1183
00:49:42,560 --> 00:49:45,285
in 2025, you also tried to bid to buy TikTok

1184
00:49:45,285 --> 00:49:47,280
through a competitive process

1185
00:49:47,920 --> 00:49:51,333
though the TikTok deal has now been finalized, yes —

1186
00:49:51,333 --> 00:49:54,269
so why did you want to buy TikTok back then?

1187
00:49:54,390 --> 00:49:55,670
because at that time,

1188
00:49:55,670 --> 00:49:58,296
when TikTok was being discussed publicly,

1189
00:49:58,296 --> 00:49:59,784
we did consider whether

1190
00:49:59,784 --> 00:50:02,761
having such a massive in-house traffic source

1191
00:50:02,761 --> 00:50:04,380
would be valuable for us.

1192
00:50:04,900 --> 00:50:08,105
having such a massive in-house traffic source —

1193
00:50:08,105 --> 00:50:11,310
would that be a highly valuable asset for us?

1194
00:50:11,670 --> 00:50:11,670
Right?

1195
00:50:12,390 --> 00:50:14,990
Adam had given many interviews during that time

1196
00:50:15,310 --> 00:50:19,131
he also mentioned that if AppLovin and TikTok were to merge,

1197
00:50:19,131 --> 00:50:22,416
the immense value they could create for each other

1198
00:50:22,416 --> 00:50:23,730
would be tremendous.

1199
00:50:24,090 --> 00:50:27,215
So, I think this is actually a very sensible

1200
00:50:27,215 --> 00:50:29,250
and sound business decision.

1201
00:50:29,900 --> 00:50:31,836
So, will you be building your own media,

1202
00:50:31,836 --> 00:50:33,620
or your own social media platforms?

1203
00:50:33,700 --> 00:50:33,700
Yes

1204
00:50:34,940 --> 00:50:36,020
Is this a public news

1205
00:50:36,100 --> 00:50:36,949
this should be public news,

1206
00:50:36,949 --> 00:50:38,420
since we've already posted open job listings.

1207
00:50:38,420 --> 00:50:41,580
Can you share what roadmap or plan is currently set?

1208
00:50:41,740 --> 00:50:47,107
we aim to build a brand-new, next-gen social media platform

1209
00:50:47,107 --> 00:50:48,310
from scratch.

1210
00:50:49,010 --> 00:50:50,799
and such a social media platform

1211
00:50:50,799 --> 00:50:53,930
could bring AppLovin a valuable in-house traffic stream.

1212
00:50:54,180 --> 00:50:55,860
Will we tread Meta's path?

1213
00:50:56,100 --> 00:50:58,680
I think our path is the reverse of Meta's — Meta

1214
00:50:58,680 --> 00:51:00,980
started off with its own organic traffic

1215
00:51:01,180 --> 00:51:02,787
once it had its own traffic,

1216
00:51:02,787 --> 00:51:06,300
it built its ad and algorithm teams on top of that traffic.

1217
00:51:06,660 --> 00:51:10,086
then it took the tech from its algorithms and ad teams

1218
00:51:10,086 --> 00:51:12,688
and applied it to third-party platforms —

1219
00:51:12,688 --> 00:51:14,910
we started on third-party platforms

1220
00:51:15,510 --> 00:51:18,974
once we achieved success on third-party platforms,

1221
00:51:18,974 --> 00:51:21,450
we then built our own traffic base

1222
00:51:21,810 --> 00:51:24,483
I think the path we've chosen is quite challenging,

1223
00:51:24,483 --> 00:51:26,730
but challenges bring their own advantages.

1224
00:51:27,130 --> 00:51:29,930
I think on our own traffic streams,

1225
00:51:30,410 --> 00:51:32,318
building an ad platform here

1226
00:51:32,318 --> 00:51:35,387
is somewhat easier than on third-party sites,

1227
00:51:35,387 --> 00:51:39,410
or at least faces less competition than those platforms do.

1228
00:51:39,650 --> 00:51:42,544
breaking through on third-party platforms is tough indeed,

1229
00:51:42,544 --> 00:51:45,440
as you face countless bidders vying for the same traffic

1230
00:51:45,760 --> 00:51:48,680
your traffic isn't reserved exclusively for yourself

1231
00:51:49,030 --> 00:51:50,293
but I think this is like a plant

1232
00:51:50,293 --> 00:51:52,110
that grows in a harsh, unforgiving environment

1233
00:51:52,670 --> 00:51:54,889
though you face many hardships,

1234
00:51:54,889 --> 00:51:58,810
these very challenges make you stronger and sturdier

1235
00:51:59,330 --> 00:51:59,330
Right?

1236
00:51:59,810 --> 00:52:00,474
I think that

1237
00:52:00,474 --> 00:52:03,522
after years of battling on these third-party platforms,

1238
00:52:03,522 --> 00:52:05,936
both our technology and our company culture

1239
00:52:05,936 --> 00:52:07,790
have become remarkably resilient.

1240
00:52:08,270 --> 00:52:12,565
so now, building such a platform with our own traffic stream —

1241
00:52:12,565 --> 00:52:16,570
I personally hold a very, very optimistic vision for it.

1242
00:52:16,690 --> 00:52:19,068
but social media is still tough to manage,

1243
00:52:19,068 --> 00:52:21,910
since its traffic is now dominated by the giants

1244
00:52:21,990 --> 00:52:21,991
Yes.

1245
00:52:23,230 --> 00:52:25,830
Do you recall what was still tough to do three years back?

1246
00:52:26,140 --> 00:52:26,700
ads, yes?

1247
00:52:26,980 --> 00:52:27,580
ads, yes

1248
00:52:27,940 --> 00:52:29,580
this might be yet another miracle in store

1249
00:52:29,660 --> 00:52:30,620
Yes, so there would be,

1250
00:52:30,780 --> 00:52:30,780
So,

1251
00:52:31,100 --> 00:52:33,057
so many will doubt us and lack faith,

1252
00:52:33,057 --> 00:52:35,320
and many will question—how could AppLovin

1253
00:52:35,320 --> 00:52:36,756
thrive in such a climate,

1254
00:52:36,756 --> 00:52:38,279
though it's not about buying.

1255
00:52:38,360 --> 00:52:41,240
What will be your next strategic focus moving on?

1256
00:52:41,300 --> 00:52:43,939
I know you're expanding your teams in Asia,

1257
00:52:43,939 --> 00:52:47,499
yes, and you'll keep hiring top talent in AI-related fields

1258
00:52:47,780 --> 00:52:50,880
Yes, what will be your next strategic focus moving forward?

1259
00:52:50,880 --> 00:52:52,588
I think there are several key points.

1260
00:52:52,588 --> 00:52:54,666
First, people tend to think of new initiatives

1261
00:52:54,666 --> 00:52:55,959
when talking about strategy.

1262
00:52:56,360 --> 00:52:59,200
But, uh, right now, I think the things we already have,

1263
00:52:59,830 --> 00:53:02,192
our existing businesses—mobile apps

1264
00:53:02,192 --> 00:53:06,026
and e-commerce—will remain a cornerstone of our strategy

1265
00:53:06,026 --> 00:53:07,054
going forward,

1266
00:53:07,054 --> 00:53:08,269
wouldn't you agree?

1267
00:53:08,270 --> 00:53:11,187
they will, for many years to come,

1268
00:53:11,187 --> 00:53:15,472
continue to shape the baseline of our company.

1269
00:53:15,472 --> 00:53:17,659
So this is clearly vital.

1270
00:53:18,220 --> 00:53:22,425
Hmm, and beyond that, we have another strategic focus

1271
00:53:22,425 --> 00:53:24,570
known as Supply Expansion —

1272
00:53:24,570 --> 00:53:28,200
essentially expanding our ad traffic volume.

1273
00:53:28,760 --> 00:53:29,800
ad traffic growth

1274
00:53:29,960 --> 00:53:31,956
and here were some things—maybe I,

1275
00:53:31,956 --> 00:53:33,859
or perhaps Adam M—had mentioned

1276
00:53:33,859 --> 00:53:36,071
as far back as one or two years ago,

1277
00:53:36,071 --> 00:53:38,310
about traffic growth on the CTV side?

1278
00:53:38,530 --> 00:53:41,264
there is another traffic-expansion plan

1279
00:53:41,264 --> 00:53:44,210
we see as quite ambitious and bold indeed

1280
00:53:44,410 --> 00:53:45,700
as you just mentioned,

1281
00:53:45,700 --> 00:53:47,573
we will start building our own

1282
00:53:47,573 --> 00:53:49,510
in-house social media platform

1283
00:53:49,830 --> 00:53:51,030
Hmm, social media site.

1284
00:53:51,620 --> 00:53:52,205
To sum up,

1285
00:53:52,205 --> 00:53:56,042
the reasons why AppLovin chose to sell its gaming business

1286
00:53:56,042 --> 00:53:59,100
may stem from deeper commercial considerations.

1287
00:53:59,370 --> 00:54:02,410
First, the Data Lab's original mission has concluded.

1288
00:54:02,730 --> 00:54:05,508
CEO Adam said in the earnings report that seven years ago

1289
00:54:05,508 --> 00:54:07,069
we began acquiring game studios,

1290
00:54:07,069 --> 00:54:09,800
which helped train our earliest Machine Learning models

1291
00:54:10,040 --> 00:54:13,360
this was a key step in building the AI our AXON relies on.

1292
00:54:13,560 --> 00:54:16,000
yet at our core, we are not game developers

1293
00:54:16,200 --> 00:54:17,815
now that AXON 2.0 is mature,

1294
00:54:17,815 --> 00:54:21,430
the algorithms can pull sufficient signals from MAX's

1295
00:54:21,430 --> 00:54:23,000
global bidding streams.

1296
00:54:23,180 --> 00:54:24,789
so-called First-Party Data —

1297
00:54:24,789 --> 00:54:27,615
the data firms gather directly from consumers

1298
00:54:27,615 --> 00:54:29,060
via their own channels —

1299
00:54:29,060 --> 00:54:30,980
now contributes almost nothing.

1300
00:54:31,100 --> 00:54:33,139
Second, the valuation reset.

1301
00:54:33,520 --> 00:54:35,444
the games business is stable,

1302
00:54:35,444 --> 00:54:39,359
yet 19% EBITOA margin is deemed 'mediocre assets' by Wall Street

1303
00:54:39,360 --> 00:54:42,129
By contrast, AppLovin's ad business —

1304
00:54:42,129 --> 00:54:46,719
its software platform — has an EBITDA margin of a stunning 81%.

1305
00:54:46,860 --> 00:54:50,693
this meant it had fully shifted from a games-plus-ads firm

1306
00:54:50,693 --> 00:54:52,940
to a premium AI software platform.

1307
00:54:53,170 --> 00:54:55,992
Third, extreme resource bets: e-commerce hosts

1308
00:54:55,992 --> 00:54:59,650
over 10 million potential advertisers, far outpacing gaming.

1309
00:54:59,780 --> 00:55:02,958
To achieve 'step-function' growth in this $100B market,

1310
00:55:02,958 --> 00:55:05,700
AppLovin must shed its bloated gaming teams

1311
00:55:05,800 --> 00:55:09,240
pour each watt of compute into e-commerce model tweaks

1312
00:55:09,520 --> 00:55:12,147
the subsequent growth in AppLovin's ad business

1313
00:55:12,147 --> 00:55:14,160
proved the shift was the right move

1314
00:55:14,160 --> 00:55:16,324
AppLovin's stock began surging sharply,

1315
00:55:16,324 --> 00:55:19,800
with more reports dubbing it the 'first AI beneficiary stock'

1316
00:55:20,120 --> 00:55:22,034
yet as the firm rose swiftly,

1317
00:55:22,034 --> 00:55:23,840
doubts and bets against it

1318
00:55:23,840 --> 00:55:26,480
from the outside never ceased to grow.

1319
00:55:30,320 --> 00:55:33,660
as AppLovin's market cap neared $200 billion,

1320
00:55:33,660 --> 00:55:37,000
three short attacks came just as expected.

1321
00:55:37,140 --> 00:55:37,686
First clash:

1322
00:55:37,686 --> 00:55:40,220
Culper and Fuzzy Panda's 'AI Camouflage Allegation'

1323
00:55:40,540 --> 00:55:41,614
On February 26, 2025,

1324
00:55:41,614 --> 00:55:43,637
the firms Culper and Fuzzy Panda

1325
00:55:43,637 --> 00:55:45,660
jointly released a major report.

1326
00:55:45,890 --> 00:55:48,316
at that time, AppLovin's stock was at a peak

1327
00:55:48,316 --> 00:55:50,010
fueled by the AXON 2.0 narrative

1328
00:55:50,290 --> 00:55:52,062
In its report, Culper claimed that

1329
00:55:52,062 --> 00:55:54,371
so-called AXON 2.0 was not advanced AI at all,

1330
00:55:54,371 --> 00:55:55,626
but a promotional patch

1331
00:55:55,626 --> 00:55:57,809
leveraging low-level system permissions.

1332
00:55:58,130 --> 00:56:00,369
the report accused the firm of misusing

1333
00:56:00,369 --> 00:56:01,977
Android install permissions,

1334
00:56:01,977 --> 00:56:03,543
using Single-click download

1335
00:56:03,543 --> 00:56:06,076
to silently install games in the background

1336
00:56:06,076 --> 00:56:07,490
without users' knowledge.

1337
00:56:07,800 --> 00:56:10,278
Fuzzy Panda further alleged that AppLovin,

1338
00:56:10,278 --> 00:56:11,971
in its push into e-commerce,

1339
00:56:11,971 --> 00:56:13,060
used illegal SDKs.

1340
00:56:13,060 --> 00:56:15,494
that is, the SDK tracked minors' data

1341
00:56:15,494 --> 00:56:19,460
and stole Meta's attribution model to inflate ad results.

1342
00:56:19,780 --> 00:56:21,873
In today's Apple privacy regime,

1343
00:56:21,873 --> 00:56:24,980
this is akin to claiming a bank spies on PINs.

1344
00:56:25,200 --> 00:56:27,516
the second clash: Muddy Waters questions

1345
00:56:27,516 --> 00:56:29,120
growth claims' authenticity.

1346
00:56:29,320 --> 00:56:31,946
If the first battle questioned technical methods,

1347
00:56:31,946 --> 00:56:33,443
then 'King of Short Selling'

1348
00:56:33,443 --> 00:56:35,996
Carson Block and Muddy Waters entered the fray,

1349
00:56:35,996 --> 00:56:38,240
targeting AppLovin's core business logic.

1350
00:56:38,700 --> 00:56:40,735
Muddy Waters' report lambasts AppLovin;

1351
00:56:40,735 --> 00:56:43,100
its 'incremental conversion' logic is flawed

1352
00:56:43,610 --> 00:56:46,616
the report, via independent audits of advertiser data,

1353
00:56:46,616 --> 00:56:48,783
claimed the true uplift from AppLovin

1354
00:56:48,783 --> 00:56:51,010
was just one-third of what it claimed

1355
00:56:51,330 --> 00:56:52,512
Muddy Waters claims

1356
00:56:52,512 --> 00:56:55,500
AppLovin cleverly merged cross-platform ID data

1357
00:56:55,500 --> 00:56:59,049
to credit natural traffic from Meta or TikTok as its own

1358
00:56:59,220 --> 00:57:01,361
thus crafting AI-driven growth illusions

1359
00:57:01,361 --> 00:57:02,700
in its financial reports.

1360
00:57:02,980 --> 00:57:05,024
this deconstruction of 'black box

1361
00:57:05,024 --> 00:57:07,580
attribution' shook investors deeply once

1362
00:57:07,820 --> 00:57:10,205
The third clash came recently with Capital Watch,

1363
00:57:10,205 --> 00:57:12,217
which accused AppLovin of 'Southeast Asia

1364
00:57:12,217 --> 00:57:14,180
money laundering' and geopolitical ties.

1365
00:57:14,340 --> 00:57:17,341
Yet soon, CapitalWatch apologized for some factual errors

1366
00:57:17,341 --> 00:57:19,110
and retracted part of its claims,

1367
00:57:19,110 --> 00:57:22,220
though it vowed to raise further questions about AppLovin.

1368
00:57:22,460 --> 00:57:24,000
these back-and-forth clashes

1369
00:57:24,000 --> 00:57:25,925
not only reveal Wall Street's deep

1370
00:57:25,925 --> 00:57:27,630
concerns over such high-growth,

1371
00:57:27,630 --> 00:57:29,500
low-transparency algorithm models

1372
00:57:29,730 --> 00:57:31,179
it also saw AppLovin's stock

1373
00:57:31,179 --> 00:57:33,250
endure extreme price swings and turmoil

1374
00:57:33,550 --> 00:57:35,470
for a very long stretch of time

1375
00:57:35,630 --> 00:57:38,580
the sellers' claims and buyers' bullish bets on AppLovin

1376
00:57:38,580 --> 00:57:40,821
clashed fiercely in the secondary market,

1377
00:57:40,821 --> 00:57:43,280
sending shares on wild swings multiple times.

1378
00:57:43,650 --> 00:57:47,313
You joined the S&P 500 Index in September

1379
00:57:47,313 --> 00:57:49,850
2025—does that matter to you?

1380
00:57:50,470 --> 00:57:52,190
Not much of an impact, to be frank.

1381
00:57:52,190 --> 00:57:53,528
But over the past year,

1382
00:57:53,528 --> 00:57:56,173
we often joked that our team would get busy

1383
00:57:56,173 --> 00:57:59,127
each time the S&P 500 Index underwent a rebalance,

1384
00:57:59,127 --> 00:58:01,440
which happened right around this time.

1385
00:58:01,840 --> 00:58:04,680
like, about a week after the earnings report?

1386
00:58:04,760 --> 00:58:06,888
once the earnings report came out,

1387
00:58:06,888 --> 00:58:10,307
our team would gather up and often discuss one topic:

1388
00:58:10,307 --> 00:58:12,630
whether we'd be added to the S&P 500

1389
00:58:12,720 --> 00:58:14,912
then folks would treat this as a bit of fun,

1390
00:58:14,912 --> 00:58:17,920
and sometimes we'd even joke around and say, 'Bet ten bucks'

1391
00:58:18,240 --> 00:58:20,535
Yes, and when it actually happened,

1392
00:58:20,535 --> 00:58:23,040
we weren't really surprised, were we?

1393
00:58:23,040 --> 00:58:26,520
although the S&P itself is decided by the committee,

1394
00:58:26,520 --> 00:58:27,835
unlike some indices

1395
00:58:27,835 --> 00:58:31,160
that are fully rule-driven in their composition.

1396
00:58:31,280 --> 00:58:32,342
but the S&P itself

1397
00:58:32,342 --> 00:58:35,000
still operates within a set of defined rules?

1398
00:58:35,120 --> 00:58:36,830
those checkboxes in the rules —

1399
00:58:36,830 --> 00:58:39,463
we had already ticked them off months before,

1400
00:58:39,463 --> 00:58:40,900
during that time period.

1401
00:58:41,020 --> 00:58:42,700
so within our team back then,

1402
00:58:42,700 --> 00:58:44,980
joining S&P was just a matter of time.

1403
00:58:45,100 --> 00:58:47,405
this means you've finally gained recognition

1404
00:58:47,405 --> 00:58:48,820
from the mainstream market

1405
00:58:49,290 --> 00:58:50,990
Not important. I truly don't care.

1406
00:58:50,990 --> 00:58:52,329
After years as an underdog,

1407
00:58:52,329 --> 00:58:53,218
we no longer care

1408
00:58:53,218 --> 00:58:55,729
whether the mainstream market accepts us or not.

1409
00:58:56,040 --> 00:58:56,046
another downside of being an underdog is that many questioned you — yes, in 2015 you faced numerous rounds of short-selling

1410
00:59:03,280 --> 00:59:06,120
I see your CEO has addressed these claims many times over

1411
00:59:06,120 --> 00:59:08,651
I still want to ask this: regarding the short-sellers,

1412
00:59:08,651 --> 00:59:10,210
especially those who target you

1413
00:59:10,730 --> 00:59:13,332
the compliance of some of your data,

1414
00:59:13,332 --> 00:59:16,503
or whether you used somewhat shady methods

1415
00:59:16,503 --> 00:59:18,240
to improve your models?

1416
00:59:18,480 --> 00:59:19,680
What is your response to this?

1417
00:59:19,880 --> 00:59:23,850
I think all those claims from the short-sellers

1418
00:59:23,850 --> 00:59:26,470
are surely completely baseless.

1419
00:59:26,830 --> 00:59:29,124
Regarding this, I can say that during the period

1420
00:59:29,124 --> 00:59:30,638
when the short report came out,

1421
00:59:30,638 --> 00:59:31,420
I played a role.

1422
00:59:31,420 --> 00:59:33,095
when this short report first came out,

1423
00:59:33,095 --> 00:59:35,179
we all took it very seriously, as did I myself.

1424
00:59:35,320 --> 00:59:36,672
so my main role back then

1425
00:59:36,672 --> 00:59:39,000
was that I carefully read the short report

1426
00:59:39,280 --> 00:59:40,072
then I checked

1427
00:59:40,072 --> 00:59:42,847
whether the technical claims in the short report

1428
00:59:42,847 --> 00:59:44,490
actually occurred in reality

1429
00:59:44,930 --> 00:59:49,960
then I checked each point one by one against our system data

1430
00:59:50,160 --> 00:59:53,208
and later on, we found that all of them were wrong —

1431
00:59:53,208 --> 00:59:54,366
once that was clear,

1432
00:59:54,366 --> 00:59:57,049
I no longer took direct part in this matter.

1433
00:59:57,090 --> 00:59:59,090
I passed this information

1434
00:59:59,090 --> 01:00:02,050
to Adam and our other PR departments.

1435
01:00:02,370 --> 01:00:04,553
then I went back to my own role and tasks,

1436
01:00:04,553 --> 01:00:07,110
and no longer followed up on this matter at all.

1437
01:00:07,390 --> 01:00:11,210
so for you all, those mistakes were indeed entirely wrong

1438
01:00:11,250 --> 01:00:12,994
I think in that context back then,

1439
01:00:12,994 --> 01:00:15,850
it was hard to argue whether they were right or wrong?

1440
01:00:16,130 --> 01:00:20,658
this is why I think we've always, when handling this matter,

1441
01:00:20,658 --> 01:00:23,470
chosen to let time speak for itself

1442
01:00:23,470 --> 01:00:24,869
once some time had passed,

1443
01:00:24,869 --> 01:00:27,928
you'd find that the claims of short-sellers against us

1444
01:00:27,928 --> 01:00:29,460
would crumble on their own

1445
01:00:29,460 --> 01:00:31,756
In 2025, as shares surged, outside

1446
01:00:31,756 --> 01:00:34,360
follow-up led to the short report.

1447
01:00:34,640 --> 01:00:35,803
so during this time,

1448
01:00:35,803 --> 01:00:38,560
did you notice any changes within yourselves?

1449
01:00:38,740 --> 01:00:44,190
I think the whole team has remained fairly stable overall.

1450
01:00:44,590 --> 01:00:46,185
when the down-report came out,

1451
01:00:46,185 --> 01:00:48,631
I was quite worried too—whether team members

1452
01:00:48,631 --> 01:00:50,300
might face any sort of impact

1453
01:00:50,300 --> 01:00:51,080
at this stage

1454
01:00:51,080 --> 01:00:51,080
Yes.

1455
01:00:51,560 --> 01:00:51,560
Right?

1456
01:00:52,300 --> 01:00:55,030
including team members—whether they might face doubts

1457
01:00:55,030 --> 01:00:57,195
from those close to them—during that time,

1458
01:00:57,195 --> 01:01:00,030
I sent some memos to the team to address such concerns

1459
01:01:00,030 --> 01:01:02,070
just to say that if you have anything

1460
01:01:02,590 --> 01:01:04,710
let me briefly explain what has been happening

1461
01:01:04,830 --> 01:01:08,800
if anyone has concerns, feel free to come and talk to me

1462
01:01:09,040 --> 01:01:12,389
in the end, not many people came to talk to me—perhaps

1463
01:01:12,389 --> 01:01:13,717
everyone on the team

1464
01:01:13,717 --> 01:01:16,120
had already grasped the issue clearly

1465
01:01:21,400 --> 01:01:24,341
AppLovin has been quite restrained in hiring talent.

1466
01:01:24,341 --> 01:01:26,130
Walking into its Palo Alto HQ,

1467
01:01:26,130 --> 01:01:27,283
the first thought is:

1468
01:01:27,283 --> 01:01:30,399
it feels very empty—few people in the entire building.

1469
01:01:30,580 --> 01:01:33,051
the company now has over 100 engineers,

1470
01:01:33,051 --> 01:01:34,929
and per the earnings report,

1471
01:01:34,929 --> 01:01:38,200
each one generates roughly $40 million in profit

1472
01:01:38,400 --> 01:01:40,971
but Giovanni Ge still faces his toughest challenge:

1473
01:01:40,971 --> 01:01:41,640
hiring staff

1474
01:01:42,020 --> 01:01:42,027
the challenge lies in sustaining an 'underdog' culture internally while the company grows fast and faces external downgrades

1475
01:01:49,780 --> 01:01:51,445
I think the biggest hurdle is

1476
01:01:51,445 --> 01:01:53,340
that the talent market doubts us

1477
01:01:53,810 --> 01:01:57,210
we found it extremely tough to hire people that year in 2023

1478
01:01:57,450 --> 01:02:00,090
because folks all want to join well-known big firms.

1479
01:02:00,370 --> 01:02:02,699
Yes, and not quite — in North America,

1480
01:02:02,699 --> 01:02:06,400
I think the diversity of talent options is quite good

1481
01:02:06,680 --> 01:02:08,121
many are also keen to join

1482
01:02:08,121 --> 01:02:10,560
small firms or startups with good prospects.

1483
01:02:10,820 --> 01:02:13,112
but in the eyes of many of our top talents,

1484
01:02:13,112 --> 01:02:15,460
we aren't even seen as a promising startup.

1485
01:02:15,940 --> 01:02:17,300
in the year 2023

1486
01:02:17,340 --> 01:02:19,340
why didn't folks see your company as promising

1487
01:02:19,340 --> 01:02:21,610
we were quite small in size

1488
01:02:21,890 --> 01:02:24,550
second, in this industry we're in — well,

1489
01:02:24,550 --> 01:02:27,840
at that time, Google and MT were seen as already

1490
01:02:27,840 --> 01:02:30,500
extremely mature and well-established?

1491
01:02:30,500 --> 01:02:30,506
had already cornered that market. Hence, folks likely saw the field as not particularly exciting. Hmm, so hiring was tough back then.

1492
01:02:37,610 --> 01:02:39,370
How did you tackle this problem back then?

1493
01:02:39,410 --> 01:02:41,720
Hmm, wait—some issues, perhaps you then,

1494
01:02:41,720 --> 01:02:43,906
and I tried many approaches myself,

1495
01:02:43,906 --> 01:02:45,780
but none worked all that well?

1496
01:02:45,940 --> 01:02:48,340
later on, I came to see it clearly

1497
01:02:48,340 --> 01:02:49,925
I felt that the most important thing then

1498
01:02:49,925 --> 01:02:51,240
was just to get the product built

1499
01:02:51,280 --> 01:02:53,788
we used some resources the team already had back then,

1500
01:02:53,788 --> 01:02:56,343
but one great thing about AppLovin is its top-to-bottom

1501
01:02:56,343 --> 01:02:57,319
technical leadership

1502
01:02:57,780 --> 01:02:59,175
Hmm, the tech leaders at all levels —

1503
01:02:59,175 --> 01:03:00,900
they are quite hands-on in their approach.

1504
01:03:00,900 --> 01:03:00,900
Morn.

1505
01:03:01,640 --> 01:03:04,568
this is something we have that many other firms,

1506
01:03:04,568 --> 01:03:07,560
including many Big Tech companies, simply do not

1507
01:03:07,600 --> 01:03:10,065
that is when we want to build something new,

1508
01:03:10,065 --> 01:03:12,699
but can't find the right talent to carry it out.

1509
01:03:13,220 --> 01:03:14,627
our top tech leader

1510
01:03:14,627 --> 01:03:18,110
can step in and personally tackle this project

1511
01:03:18,110 --> 01:03:18,750
wrote code myself

1512
01:03:18,830 --> 01:03:20,001
Yes, wrote the code ourselves, right?

1513
01:03:20,001 --> 01:03:21,339
So that was one choice we made back then.

1514
01:03:21,980 --> 01:03:23,372
Of course, after that, right?

1515
01:03:23,372 --> 01:03:25,246
We achieved some level of success.

1516
01:03:25,246 --> 01:03:27,870
Then, our—well, we gained recognition in the market,

1517
01:03:27,870 --> 01:03:29,959
and our stock prices rose quite nicely.

1518
01:03:30,280 --> 01:03:33,596
and once our financial footing became more solid,

1519
01:03:33,596 --> 01:03:36,124
our ability to offer competitive pay

1520
01:03:36,124 --> 01:03:37,880
for talent also improved.

1521
01:03:38,040 --> 01:03:41,440
so after 2024, we made a few key changes in our path

1522
01:03:41,440 --> 01:03:43,310
One was that our pay rates

1523
01:03:43,310 --> 01:03:46,188
were the highest across Silicon Valley.

1524
01:03:46,188 --> 01:03:48,777
Of course, once AI emerged, the bubble —

1525
01:03:48,777 --> 01:03:51,079
yes — came crashing over us again.

1526
01:03:51,200 --> 01:03:53,094
but outside the AI realm,

1527
01:03:53,094 --> 01:03:57,910
we remain the top-paying firm in all of Silicon Valley today.

1528
01:03:58,310 --> 01:04:00,150
and another key shift back then

1529
01:04:00,150 --> 01:04:01,990
was that we completely dropped

1530
01:04:02,230 --> 01:04:06,649
the industry's focus on seasoned candidates shifted entirely —

1531
01:04:06,649 --> 01:04:09,080
our hiring now centers elsewhere.

1532
01:04:09,360 --> 01:04:13,350
new hires include those who just graduated from college,

1533
01:04:13,350 --> 01:04:17,050
or have less than two years of industry experience

1534
01:04:17,290 --> 01:04:18,930
Why did we make this particular shift then?

1535
01:04:19,170 --> 01:04:21,270
we found, which aspect did you shift?

1536
01:04:21,550 --> 01:04:24,779
it was a new pay shift—senior industry folks were dropped,

1537
01:04:24,779 --> 01:04:26,480
as they were tough to recruit

1538
01:04:26,600 --> 01:04:28,418
Hmm, there are many factors.

1539
01:04:28,418 --> 01:04:30,639
One key point I noticed this year

1540
01:04:30,639 --> 01:04:33,939
is that the value of Experience is overestimated.

1541
01:04:34,420 --> 01:04:36,450
This finding is key.

1542
01:04:36,450 --> 01:04:36,450
Yes

1543
01:04:36,890 --> 01:04:38,650
Why so, how did you come to this view?

1544
01:04:38,850 --> 01:04:41,887
you realize that if you run a company

1545
01:04:41,887 --> 01:04:44,186
and want to solve a problem

1546
01:04:44,186 --> 01:04:48,620
you believe has already been addressed by other firms

1547
01:04:48,700 --> 01:04:51,452
the most direct approach you might instinctively take

1548
01:04:51,452 --> 01:04:53,034
is to poach a Director—someone

1549
01:04:53,034 --> 01:04:54,933
who excels at this—from the company

1550
01:04:54,933 --> 01:04:56,960
that's best at solving this very issue

1551
01:04:57,210 --> 01:04:59,370
have him rebuild the team

1552
01:04:59,930 --> 01:05:01,370
then the issue gets solved

1553
01:05:01,730 --> 01:05:04,986
but if you look closely through history for such a case,

1554
01:05:04,986 --> 01:05:07,688
you'll find that most of these kinds of cases

1555
01:05:07,688 --> 01:05:09,130
actually end up failing.

1556
01:05:09,680 --> 01:05:12,240
There are many reasons here, but I won't go through them all.

1557
01:05:12,460 --> 01:05:13,678
go through it once more.

1558
01:05:13,678 --> 01:05:14,642
But I believe that

1559
01:05:14,642 --> 01:05:16,946
truly outstanding people—what matters most

1560
01:05:16,946 --> 01:05:18,339
is their learning ability.

1561
01:05:18,620 --> 01:05:20,316
but all these past experiences don't reflect

1562
01:05:20,316 --> 01:05:21,725
one's learning skills—they just show

1563
01:05:21,725 --> 01:05:22,900
what the person already knows.

1564
01:05:23,580 --> 01:05:26,248
but in truth, I don't need to hire someone

1565
01:05:26,248 --> 01:05:27,616
just to do something

1566
01:05:27,616 --> 01:05:29,440
they already know how to do

1567
01:05:29,480 --> 01:05:31,788
What matters more is that the person you hire

1568
01:05:31,788 --> 01:05:32,660
can grow quickly

1569
01:05:32,660 --> 01:05:35,020
and figure out things they didn't know before

1570
01:05:35,180 --> 01:05:38,107
the value of this far surpasses the knowledge

1571
01:05:38,107 --> 01:05:41,100
already stored and crystallized in one's mind

1572
01:05:41,620 --> 01:05:43,859
so new hires tend to have strong growth potential.

1573
01:05:44,020 --> 01:05:44,691
And then, ah,

1574
01:05:44,691 --> 01:05:47,743
as for the way we approach things here — we want to,

1575
01:05:47,743 --> 01:05:49,880
we aim to break away from the norm.

1576
01:05:50,020 --> 01:05:52,286
Often, such pre-existing knowledge

1577
01:05:52,286 --> 01:05:54,346
ends up becoming a hindrance.

1578
01:05:54,346 --> 01:05:57,779
Hence, starting in 2024, our hiring philosophy shifted.

1579
01:05:58,020 --> 01:06:00,260
I want to challenge you on one specific point

1580
01:06:00,300 --> 01:06:03,677
but I think AppLovin's ad campaign taking off wasn't

1581
01:06:03,677 --> 01:06:06,340
mainly because we hired you specifically

1582
01:06:06,380 --> 01:06:07,197
and did the skill set

1583
01:06:07,197 --> 01:06:09,300
you had help drive this business forward successfully?

1584
01:06:09,300 --> 01:06:11,718
Yes, so I often joke about myself —

1585
01:06:11,718 --> 01:06:13,330
I'd never hire myself.

1586
01:06:13,330 --> 01:06:16,187
I feel lucky that I joined this company

1587
01:06:16,187 --> 01:06:18,459
before I even came to AppLovin.

1588
01:06:18,660 --> 01:06:19,180
As for me

1589
01:06:19,220 --> 01:06:21,647
because the stages were different—back then

1590
01:06:21,647 --> 01:06:23,511
you were building something new,

1591
01:06:23,511 --> 01:06:25,939
so you needed the skill set you already had

1592
01:06:26,100 --> 01:06:27,412
but now you have a methodology,

1593
01:06:27,412 --> 01:06:28,900
so scaling is what you need to do

1594
01:06:28,900 --> 01:06:31,700
so hiring bright young folks would be quite reasonable

1595
01:06:31,740 --> 01:06:32,802
Yes, I think that

1596
01:06:32,802 --> 01:06:36,190
relying solely on folks with zero prior experience...

1597
01:06:36,550 --> 01:06:39,232
Hmm, if you try to build a team with folks

1598
01:06:39,232 --> 01:06:40,868
who have zero experience,

1599
01:06:40,868 --> 01:06:44,010
then your team truly needs a seed, a seed player?

1600
01:06:44,240 --> 01:06:47,237
this seed player could be someone with vast experience,

1601
01:06:47,237 --> 01:06:49,099
or someone with little experience

1602
01:06:49,099 --> 01:06:50,680
but who learns very quickly.

1603
01:06:50,840 --> 01:06:52,082
That could work too.

1604
01:06:52,082 --> 01:06:53,822
When I first joined AppLovin,

1605
01:06:53,822 --> 01:06:57,489
I didn't know most of the things I ended up learning there

1606
01:06:58,210 --> 01:07:00,772
I figured things out myself through hands-on practice

1607
01:07:00,772 --> 01:07:01,450
once I joined

1608
01:07:02,010 --> 01:07:05,466
so I think the roughly ten years of industry experience

1609
01:07:05,466 --> 01:07:08,545
I had before joining AppLovin was overestimated —

1610
01:07:08,545 --> 01:07:10,870
I don't think it held that much value

1611
01:07:11,030 --> 01:07:14,263
If I had just spent two years in the industry, say at Meta,

1612
01:07:14,263 --> 01:07:16,590
I think I could have done similar things.

1613
01:07:16,830 --> 01:07:18,630
What traits do you think set you apart here

1614
01:07:19,190 --> 01:07:21,880
and that is what makes you the right person,

1615
01:07:21,880 --> 01:07:24,999
then led 'up loving' to thrive over these three years.

1616
01:07:25,400 --> 01:07:26,640
I think it is, hmm,

1617
01:07:27,150 --> 01:07:29,190
just dare to challenge the norm

1618
01:07:29,190 --> 01:07:32,285
when doing things—don't stick rigidly to rules,

1619
01:07:32,285 --> 01:07:34,283
and don't copy Google and Meta

1620
01:07:34,283 --> 01:07:38,080
just because they're the undisputed giants in this field.

1621
01:07:38,560 --> 01:07:42,000
and I have always reminded myself and urged my team as well

1622
01:07:42,000 --> 01:07:45,190
since we don't have as many resources as Google and Meta,

1623
01:07:45,190 --> 01:07:48,380
we simply can't use their methods to solve our problems

1624
01:07:48,780 --> 01:07:50,172
Yes, in fact, I've noticed

1625
01:07:50,172 --> 01:07:52,872
that many firms in the industry—be they in ads

1626
01:07:52,872 --> 01:07:55,280
or other sectors—follow similar patterns

1627
01:07:55,640 --> 01:07:57,720
I think these firms often approach things in this way,

1628
01:07:58,180 --> 01:08:00,904
Hmm, a common mistake is that they copy Big Tech,

1629
01:08:00,904 --> 01:08:03,860
and mimic the ways Big Tech uses to solve problems.

1630
01:08:04,140 --> 01:08:07,500
yet failed to realize they lack the resources Big Tech has

1631
01:08:07,580 --> 01:08:09,019
if you lack the resources of Big Tech

1632
01:08:09,019 --> 01:08:10,925
yet still try to solve problems as Big Tech does,

1633
01:08:10,925 --> 01:08:11,820
you are doomed to fail.

1634
01:08:11,960 --> 01:08:14,617
So AppLovin will surely grow larger over time,

1635
01:08:14,617 --> 01:08:17,665
yes—but so far, I see that you remain quite cautious

1636
01:08:17,665 --> 01:08:19,520
when it comes to hiring people

1637
01:08:19,680 --> 01:08:22,305
though you are now a company with a huge market cap,

1638
01:08:22,305 --> 01:08:23,800
I assume you have ample cash.

1639
01:08:24,029 --> 01:08:27,510
and how do you approach growth as you scale up

1640
01:08:27,939 --> 01:08:30,999
will you keep 'restraint' as a core value

1641
01:08:30,999 --> 01:08:32,805
across the whole team,

1642
01:08:32,805 --> 01:08:36,179
and how do you avoid falling into the traps

1643
01:08:36,179 --> 01:08:37,749
of big corporations?

1644
01:08:37,750 --> 01:08:40,310
I think 'restraint' is a term you used most aptly and well

1645
01:08:40,590 --> 01:08:43,984
we aren't trying to keep the team tiny at all—we are hiring,

1646
01:08:43,984 --> 01:08:45,710
and we keep hiring constantly

1647
01:08:45,830 --> 01:08:48,069
but we have indeed stayed self-restrained

1648
01:08:48,390 --> 01:08:50,064
we won't hire just to hire,

1649
01:08:50,064 --> 01:08:52,234
and we won't squander our resources

1650
01:08:52,234 --> 01:08:54,469
just because we have enough of them.

1651
01:08:54,819 --> 01:08:55,975
because in my view,

1652
01:08:55,975 --> 01:08:59,899
when a team becomes overly bloated or just too large in size,

1653
01:09:00,290 --> 01:09:02,478
the extra pay you hand out

1654
01:09:02,478 --> 01:09:06,770
is just a tiny fraction of all the losses you face

1655
01:09:07,930 --> 01:09:11,050
once a team becomes bloated, it harms the existing team

1656
01:09:11,450 --> 01:09:14,698
the impact on future iteration speeds

1657
01:09:14,698 --> 01:09:17,421
and the opportunity costs lost

1658
01:09:17,421 --> 01:09:20,319
will grow by orders of magnitude.

1659
01:09:20,880 --> 01:09:22,661
so from this perspective,

1660
01:09:22,661 --> 01:09:25,481
yes — this is why we truly wish to stay,

1661
01:09:25,481 --> 01:09:27,113
to remain restrained —

1662
01:09:27,113 --> 01:09:28,448
that is our motive?

1663
01:09:28,689 --> 01:09:32,933
and as for how to avoid repeating the mistakes

1664
01:09:32,933 --> 01:09:36,439
that other big firms have made before

1665
01:09:36,520 --> 01:09:39,685
I'm quite puzzled too—everyone knows this path is wrong,

1666
01:09:39,685 --> 01:09:40,871
yet so many companies

1667
01:09:40,871 --> 01:09:43,358
keep making the same mistake time and again.

1668
01:09:43,510 --> 01:09:45,750
because with more people, politics is bound to come.

1669
01:09:46,069 --> 01:09:48,483
Right? A person might just slip

1670
01:09:48,483 --> 01:09:50,979
and take that first wrong step.

1671
01:09:51,180 --> 01:09:52,790
because I think most companies

1672
01:09:52,790 --> 01:09:54,723
where the founders are still around

1673
01:09:54,723 --> 01:09:56,281
tend to be quite restrained,

1674
01:09:56,281 --> 01:09:59,019
but you might have taken that first step by mistake

1675
01:09:59,520 --> 01:10:02,390
took that first step and made the wrong choice

1676
01:10:02,390 --> 01:10:03,546
then the team grew larger;

1677
01:10:03,546 --> 01:10:04,880
once it grew, you lost control,

1678
01:10:04,880 --> 01:10:07,148
and once you lost control, it just kept growing more

1679
01:10:07,670 --> 01:10:09,232
we'll do our best

1680
01:10:09,232 --> 01:10:13,460
to avoid making that first mistake altogether.

1681
01:10:13,700 --> 01:10:15,220
Things are fine now; I think things are

1682
01:10:15,540 --> 01:10:17,397
Hmm, across our company,

1683
01:10:17,397 --> 01:10:20,905
no one treats the headcount in their team

1684
01:10:20,905 --> 01:10:23,730
as a metric worth boasting about.

1685
01:10:24,210 --> 01:10:27,510
So, I myself don't want the team to become overly bloated.

1686
01:10:27,510 --> 01:10:28,454
My subordinates,

1687
01:10:28,454 --> 01:10:29,810
the managers under me,

1688
01:10:29,810 --> 01:10:33,170
also don't wish for these teams to grow excessively large.

1689
01:10:33,290 --> 01:10:35,819
so culturally, we still have a fair degree

1690
01:10:35,819 --> 01:10:38,720
of resistance to teams becoming overly bloated.

1691
01:10:38,760 --> 01:10:42,680
After 2025, will you find it easier to hire people?

1692
01:10:42,940 --> 01:10:45,858
Hmm, it is somewhat easier, somewhat easier indeed.

1693
01:10:45,858 --> 01:10:48,002
But then, because our hiring criteria

1694
01:10:48,002 --> 01:10:51,219
still differ from those of many firms in the industry.

1695
01:10:51,740 --> 01:10:52,580
What are your criteria?

1696
01:10:52,860 --> 01:10:54,240
when it comes to talent,

1697
01:10:54,240 --> 01:10:56,063
I think most firms when hiring

1698
01:10:56,063 --> 01:10:58,800
would naturally aim to recruit the very best

1699
01:10:58,920 --> 01:11:00,957
but how we judge the very top talents —

1700
01:11:00,957 --> 01:11:02,719
I think that varies quite a bit.

1701
01:11:02,800 --> 01:11:04,107
in this AI talent race,

1702
01:11:04,107 --> 01:11:07,234
you'll find that many firms truly prefer to seek out

1703
01:11:07,234 --> 01:11:09,220
those who stand in the spotlight

1704
01:11:09,220 --> 01:11:10,300
Yes, strong pedigree.

1705
01:11:10,300 --> 01:11:14,073
with strong academic backgrounds and some media exposure,

1706
01:11:14,073 --> 01:11:16,230
having published notable papers

1707
01:11:16,430 --> 01:11:20,432
but many of these spotlight talents are overhyped,

1708
01:11:20,432 --> 01:11:24,600
I think, because so many firms want to recruit them

1709
01:11:25,120 --> 01:11:27,280
they might then be Overpriced

1710
01:11:27,570 --> 01:11:29,507
but there might be one more key point:

1711
01:11:29,507 --> 01:11:31,550
these individuals under the spotlight,

1712
01:11:31,550 --> 01:11:33,487
I think they lack the Underdog spirit

1713
01:11:33,487 --> 01:11:34,849
that AppLovin truly needs

1714
01:11:35,430 --> 01:11:37,646
we actually focus more on those talents

1715
01:11:37,646 --> 01:11:39,750
who sit at the edge of the spotlight.

1716
01:11:40,490 --> 01:11:42,104
they are quite close to the spotlight,

1717
01:11:42,104 --> 01:11:43,370
and they are truly excellent.

1718
01:11:43,730 --> 01:11:44,570
I think they as well

1719
01:11:44,810 --> 01:11:46,233
from a statistical standpoint,

1720
01:11:46,233 --> 01:11:48,737
they're not much worse than those in the spotlight,

1721
01:11:48,737 --> 01:11:51,340
but they might be more reliable — that underdog spirit

1722
01:11:51,660 --> 01:11:53,603
or perhaps they never sought the spotlight

1723
01:11:53,603 --> 01:11:54,483
in the first place —

1724
01:11:54,483 --> 01:11:55,610
and that's precisely why

1725
01:11:55,610 --> 01:11:57,630
these folks are who we follow most closely.

1726
01:11:57,970 --> 01:12:00,286
thanks to our growth over the past few years,

1727
01:12:00,286 --> 01:12:02,581
we've been able to attract such individuals

1728
01:12:02,581 --> 01:12:04,130
and convince them to join us.

1729
01:12:04,410 --> 01:12:06,482
how to spot those who are underdogs

1730
01:12:06,482 --> 01:12:08,377
on the fringes of the spotlight

1731
01:12:08,377 --> 01:12:11,339
yet still highly talented — no efficient way exists

1732
01:12:11,340 --> 01:12:13,988
No super-efficient method—just talking

1733
01:12:13,988 --> 01:12:15,940
one-on-one, person by person.

1734
01:12:15,940 --> 01:12:17,383
we are also working hard

1735
01:12:17,383 --> 01:12:20,510
to build our brand and employer image in the market

1736
01:12:20,670 --> 01:12:21,884
we need to find ways

1737
01:12:21,884 --> 01:12:24,677
to clearly convey to our potential candidates

1738
01:12:24,677 --> 01:12:28,320
what kind of company we are and what kind of people we seek.

1739
01:12:28,390 --> 01:12:30,422
this often draws in candidates

1740
01:12:30,422 --> 01:12:32,590
who reach out to us voluntarily

1741
01:12:32,710 --> 01:12:34,272
In interviews, what single question

1742
01:12:34,272 --> 01:12:35,790
do you value most when assessing?

1743
01:12:35,790 --> 01:12:37,877
The question I value most

1744
01:12:37,877 --> 01:12:41,300
is whether candidates often make choices

1745
01:12:41,300 --> 01:12:44,390
that stand out from the norm in life.

1746
01:12:44,950 --> 01:12:46,910
What does this mean?

1747
01:12:47,180 --> 01:12:49,460
for instance, I had an offer from a big firm

1748
01:12:49,460 --> 01:12:50,900
I won't, I'll start up

1749
01:12:50,900 --> 01:12:50,901
This counts as one, yes

1750
01:12:52,260 --> 01:12:54,494
for instance, in the Gaokao system in China,

1751
01:12:54,494 --> 01:12:56,007
when you were in high school,

1752
01:12:56,007 --> 01:12:57,900
you got into a top-tier university

1753
01:12:58,020 --> 01:13:02,660
you also had some extracurricular hobbies?

1754
01:13:02,660 --> 01:13:04,714
or you might have done some sports,

1755
01:13:04,714 --> 01:13:06,182
or perhaps engaged in, uh,

1756
01:13:06,182 --> 01:13:09,610
some, like, charitable or philanthropy-related activities?

1757
01:13:09,810 --> 01:13:11,209
then I would be quite curious —

1758
01:13:11,209 --> 01:13:12,816
back then, everyone saw the Gaokao

1759
01:13:12,816 --> 01:13:14,375
as the most vital thing in life

1760
01:13:14,375 --> 01:13:16,130
and poured all their energy into it

1761
01:13:16,320 --> 01:13:18,480
what drove you to do other things as well

1762
01:13:18,600 --> 01:13:19,650
those tiny details

1763
01:13:19,650 --> 01:13:22,159
where you can see that when making choices,

1764
01:13:22,159 --> 01:13:24,494
he infused his own deliberate thinking —

1765
01:13:24,494 --> 01:13:26,769
that is the kind of person I value most.

1766
01:13:26,890 --> 01:13:29,170
I value this point very highly indeed.

1767
01:13:29,250 --> 01:13:31,886
one thing I was quite curious about

1768
01:13:31,886 --> 01:13:34,900
was, right after you graduated from USTC

1769
01:13:35,200 --> 01:13:36,666
many went to study in the US,

1770
01:13:36,666 --> 01:13:39,860
since folks thought that for PhDs, the US was the top choice,

1771
01:13:39,860 --> 01:13:40,959
yet you went to Italy

1772
01:13:41,320 --> 01:13:44,190
this choice was truly quite distinctive

1773
01:13:44,190 --> 01:13:44,663
Yeah, yeah.

1774
01:13:44,663 --> 01:13:46,983
But many of these things, I think, are just chance

1775
01:13:46,983 --> 01:13:48,356
occurrences in people's lives.

1776
01:13:48,356 --> 01:13:50,629
I don't think we should read too much into them?

1777
01:13:50,790 --> 01:13:51,877
when you graduated,

1778
01:13:51,877 --> 01:13:55,020
did you find Europe more appealing to you back then?

1779
01:13:55,060 --> 01:13:57,225
then you gave up many things for the US

1780
01:13:57,225 --> 01:13:58,670
and chose to go to Europe

1781
01:13:58,670 --> 01:14:00,869
I think back then, in that context,

1782
01:14:00,869 --> 01:14:04,470
it was a rather random choice and not very indicative

1783
01:14:04,590 --> 01:14:07,140
Do you think a person might be like that, right?

1784
01:14:07,140 --> 01:14:08,713
Like, when making choices, does

1785
01:14:08,713 --> 01:14:10,559
one tend to stand out quite a lot?

1786
01:14:10,560 --> 01:14:14,026
Do you think your time studying and living in Europe

1787
01:14:14,026 --> 01:14:15,560
changed you in any way?

1788
01:14:15,840 --> 01:14:20,338
there might be some, there might be some. For

1789
01:14:20,338 --> 01:14:24,939
I think life in Europe is more diverse indeed.

1790
01:14:25,220 --> 01:14:26,416
in such an environment,

1791
01:14:26,416 --> 01:14:28,183
I feel it might have inspired me

1792
01:14:28,183 --> 01:14:29,841
to embrace more possibilities

1793
01:14:29,841 --> 01:14:32,560
when making choices and thinking through problems

1794
01:14:32,760 --> 01:14:35,096
I think Italy is a truly remarkable place,

1795
01:14:35,096 --> 01:14:37,490
one steeped in a rich and ancient history

1796
01:14:37,730 --> 01:14:37,730
Yes.

1797
01:14:38,410 --> 01:14:42,429
very romantic, yet feels a tad sluggish at times.

1798
01:14:43,070 --> 01:14:45,590
What does Italy mean to you in your own view?

1799
01:14:45,790 --> 01:14:47,156
I think for me, Italy —

1800
01:14:47,156 --> 01:14:48,782
well, Italy feels like, hmm,

1801
01:14:48,782 --> 01:14:51,189
half of my hometown, wouldn't you say?

1802
01:14:51,190 --> 01:14:54,825
I think that during those years when I was in my twenties,

1803
01:14:54,825 --> 01:14:55,846
living in Italy,

1804
01:14:55,846 --> 01:14:58,909
I experienced a truly wonderful time in my life.

1805
01:14:59,080 --> 01:15:04,110
and during that time, I learned a different perspective

1806
01:15:04,110 --> 01:15:07,930
on the various choices one makes in life

1807
01:15:07,930 --> 01:15:09,547
You say Italy is lazy —

1808
01:15:09,547 --> 01:15:13,001
well, I think stereotypes aren't really 'a thing,'

1809
01:15:13,001 --> 01:15:13,589
are they?

1810
01:15:13,590 --> 01:15:16,326
people tend to fall into, fall quite deeply

1811
01:15:16,326 --> 01:15:19,780
into, a classic case of confirmation bias quite often.

1812
01:15:20,060 --> 01:15:24,090
In fact, many lazy folks can be found worldwide as well.

1813
01:15:24,130 --> 01:15:25,965
if you look at the overall stats,

1814
01:15:25,965 --> 01:15:27,011
perhaps on average,

1815
01:15:27,011 --> 01:15:30,440
Italians might indeed be a bit lazier than folks elsewhere.

1816
01:15:30,440 --> 01:15:32,118
but on an individual basis,

1817
01:15:32,118 --> 01:15:34,636
not every Italian is necessarily lazy —

1818
01:15:34,636 --> 01:15:36,959
we do have some staff based in Italy.

1819
01:15:37,100 --> 01:15:39,890
AppLovin staff in Italy work with high intensity

1820
01:15:39,890 --> 01:15:42,390
and maintain excellent productivity levels.

1821
01:15:42,550 --> 01:15:44,630
Since you came to Silicon Valley,

1822
01:15:44,630 --> 01:15:47,230
do you feel it changed you in some ways?

1823
01:15:47,480 --> 01:15:49,659
Yes, yes, I think Silicon Valley is...

1824
01:15:49,659 --> 01:15:52,479
I have lived in quite a few different places?

1825
01:15:52,600 --> 01:15:54,353
especially in Italy—yes, I lived there,

1826
01:15:54,353 --> 01:15:56,382
and to this day I still talk with my friends

1827
01:15:56,382 --> 01:15:57,259
who are from Italy.

1828
01:15:57,660 --> 01:15:58,785
Hmm, when we chat,

1829
01:15:58,785 --> 01:16:01,785
they feel that their lives are far removed

1830
01:16:01,785 --> 01:16:03,428
from the lofty heights

1831
01:16:03,428 --> 01:16:05,820
of being able to change the world.

1832
01:16:05,900 --> 01:16:07,128
so they never think about it,

1833
01:16:07,128 --> 01:16:08,620
never even consider this question

1834
01:16:08,920 --> 01:16:12,546
I think the biggest lesson Silicon Valley taught me

1835
01:16:12,546 --> 01:16:14,680
is that every ordinary person

1836
01:16:15,150 --> 01:16:19,339
the gap between him and the power to change the world

1837
01:16:19,339 --> 01:16:21,790
is actually quite small indeed.

1838
01:16:21,950 --> 01:16:23,070
What you need is just,

1839
01:16:23,490 --> 01:16:27,170
it's the courage to take that first step,

1840
01:16:27,170 --> 01:16:31,310
and then 365 days of hard work after that step?

1841
01:16:31,350 --> 01:16:34,470
this is why you just now asked me a lot about

1842
01:16:34,470 --> 01:16:36,759
whether there was one key moment

1843
01:16:36,759 --> 01:16:38,910
or one right move for AppLovin?

1844
01:16:38,990 --> 01:16:42,750
I don't think this is a key reason Silicon Valley thrived

1845
01:16:42,840 --> 01:16:44,482
once many things succeed,

1846
01:16:44,482 --> 01:16:45,594
people look back

1847
01:16:45,594 --> 01:16:49,000
and wish to pinpoint one or two standout moments.

1848
01:16:49,440 --> 01:16:53,366
but the true Silicon Valley success stems from daily efforts,

1849
01:16:53,366 --> 01:16:55,460
not just those standout moments.

1850
01:16:55,780 --> 01:16:57,534
Hmm, that was said quite well.

1851
01:16:57,534 --> 01:16:59,529
Uh, why do you ride a bike to work?

1852
01:16:59,960 --> 01:17:01,559
I don't ride my bike to work each day

1853
01:17:01,800 --> 01:17:03,628
Hmm, I—I live near the office;

1854
01:17:03,628 --> 01:17:06,109
on a good day, it takes about 30 minutes,

1855
01:17:06,109 --> 01:17:09,570
but in traffic, it could stretch to 50 or even 60 minutes.

1856
01:17:09,890 --> 01:17:13,246
sometimes, if my work hours clash with rush hour traffic,

1857
01:17:13,246 --> 01:17:15,810
I might opt for a motorcycle to save time.

1858
01:17:16,120 --> 01:17:17,960
So you take US Route 101?

1859
01:17:18,000 --> 01:17:18,520
two eight zero.

1860
01:17:18,520 --> 01:17:18,520
280

1861
01:17:19,360 --> 01:17:20,880
the view on 180 is lovely.

1862
01:17:21,560 --> 01:17:24,592
that lake at Crystal Spring—what's it like

1863
01:17:24,592 --> 01:17:26,470
to speed along Highway 280?

1864
01:17:26,470 --> 01:17:27,150
no speeding

1865
01:17:27,150 --> 01:17:29,430
just ride a bike

1866
01:17:29,430 --> 01:17:33,110
Yeah, about 70 mph usually—feels quite pleasant to me indeed

1867
01:17:33,110 --> 01:17:34,593
the wind would brush past both sides,

1868
01:17:34,593 --> 01:17:36,900
and you could hear it through the inside of your helmet.

1869
01:17:37,060 --> 01:17:38,380
Does fog often form on 280?

1870
01:17:38,500 --> 01:17:40,616
Aren't there mountains on both sides of 280,

1871
01:17:40,616 --> 01:17:42,179
with mist drifting through them?

1872
01:17:42,390 --> 01:17:44,760
and when you ride the bike, your view is much clearer,

1873
01:17:44,760 --> 01:17:47,346
and you can sense the things on both your left and right —

1874
01:17:47,346 --> 01:17:48,270
that's great indeed

1875
01:17:48,350 --> 01:17:50,910
Are you someone who enjoys speed to some extent?

1876
01:17:51,290 --> 01:17:52,274
Hmm, not bad.

1877
01:17:52,274 --> 01:17:56,459
I am someone—actually, not a reckless person at all.

1878
01:17:56,459 --> 01:17:57,854
I love adventures,

1879
01:17:57,854 --> 01:18:01,958
but the risks I take are usually Calculated Risks?

1880
01:18:02,120 --> 01:18:04,240
If I ride a bike, I won't go all that fast.

1881
01:18:04,240 --> 01:18:07,960
you could just drive along quite easily, say, at 80 mph

1882
01:18:08,130 --> 01:18:11,561
you wouldn't even feel that if you drove an SUV at 80 mph,

1883
01:18:11,561 --> 01:18:12,810
it feels quite slow

1884
01:18:12,990 --> 01:18:12,994
but if you ride a bike at 70 mph, you can definitely feel how fast that speed is, right? Hmm.

1885
01:18:17,750 --> 01:18:20,420
so feeling the speed makes you enjoy that sense of motion

1886
01:18:20,420 --> 01:18:20,980
Yes, yes indeed.

1887
01:18:21,020 --> 01:18:23,414
I heard your job is quite a rat race;

1888
01:18:23,414 --> 01:18:25,419
you reply late at night too. Yes,

1889
01:18:25,540 --> 01:18:26,627
I, I always tell others,

1890
01:18:26,627 --> 01:18:28,130
I say we don't do the rat race —

1891
01:18:28,130 --> 01:18:30,336
we don't make you join the rat race at all.

1892
01:18:30,336 --> 01:18:31,819
I think we're just hardworking.

1893
01:18:32,270 --> 01:18:33,400
OK, what's the difference,

1894
01:18:33,400 --> 01:18:34,349
rat race vs. diligence?

1895
01:18:34,590 --> 01:18:35,470
What do you see as the gap?

1896
01:18:35,750 --> 01:18:40,090
I think 'rat race' is more about what the environment demands,

1897
01:18:40,090 --> 01:18:43,990
while 'diligence' is about what one demands of oneself

1898
01:18:43,990 --> 01:18:45,817
So in that sense, we are a diligent firm,

1899
01:18:45,817 --> 01:18:47,270
not a 'rat race' kind of company

1900
01:18:47,590 --> 01:18:49,502
We never require clock-ins,

1901
01:18:49,502 --> 01:18:52,740
and we never impose strict work-hour limits?

1902
01:18:52,980 --> 01:18:56,703
but many people are— I think it's because they, like me,

1903
01:18:56,703 --> 01:19:00,990
feel a deep and genuine eagerness about the work we're doing

1904
01:19:01,310 --> 01:19:04,050
so they would choose to do so

1905
01:19:04,050 --> 01:19:04,610
very hardworking

1906
01:19:04,850 --> 01:19:08,104
you mentioned before that, indeed from a young age,

1907
01:19:08,104 --> 01:19:10,960
I know you might not like the term 'genius'

1908
01:19:10,960 --> 01:19:13,924
but you were indeed smarter than most from a young age

1909
01:19:13,924 --> 01:19:16,670
and joined USTC's Special Class for Gifted Youths

1910
01:19:16,710 --> 01:19:16,710
Right?

1911
01:19:17,510 --> 01:19:21,668
then you went to Italy for your PhD at a young age.

1912
01:19:21,668 --> 01:19:26,041
You mentioned that the period before you joined Meta

1913
01:19:26,041 --> 01:19:29,070
was, indeed, a low point in your life.

1914
01:19:29,590 --> 01:19:32,270
Yes, I did voice that view in certain settings indeed

1915
01:19:32,270 --> 01:19:36,740
Do you think, will I just end up as an ordinary person?

1916
01:19:36,740 --> 01:19:39,442
not the person others expected of you as a child,

1917
01:19:39,442 --> 01:19:41,300
nor the one you thought you were.

1918
01:19:41,600 --> 01:19:43,960
Do you think why there's such a gap like this?

1919
01:19:44,360 --> 01:19:45,295
I think for me,

1920
01:19:45,295 --> 01:19:48,006
the first major turning point in my life

1921
01:19:48,006 --> 01:19:50,040
was when I started university.

1922
01:19:50,080 --> 01:19:53,017
I think the biggest gap I had back then

1923
01:19:53,017 --> 01:19:55,654
was that, after starting university,

1924
01:19:55,654 --> 01:19:57,990
I lacked solid career guidance.

1925
01:19:58,200 --> 01:19:59,377
when I started college,

1926
01:19:59,377 --> 01:20:02,000
I had immense uncertainty about my future career.

1927
01:20:02,000 --> 01:20:03,011
I studied physics,

1928
01:20:03,011 --> 01:20:05,868
and back then I wondered—why did I pick physics,

1929
01:20:05,868 --> 01:20:08,190
and what kind of jobs could it lead to?

1930
01:20:08,870 --> 01:20:11,019
I was deeply puzzled back then;

1931
01:20:11,019 --> 01:20:13,276
due to various personal reasons

1932
01:20:13,276 --> 01:20:15,462
and some external constraints,

1933
01:20:15,462 --> 01:20:17,970
I never found satisfactory answers.

1934
01:20:18,400 --> 01:20:23,267
that one impact had a very deep and lasting negative effect

1935
01:20:23,267 --> 01:20:25,000
on my life afterward

1936
01:20:25,280 --> 01:20:25,284
indeed, because of this, I have always been like this now

1937
01:20:30,010 --> 01:20:32,789
I hope that through my own life, this life, this phase,

1938
01:20:32,789 --> 01:20:35,890
these various experiences I've gone through in this stage.

1939
01:20:36,170 --> 01:20:37,871
I hope to turn things around

1940
01:20:37,871 --> 01:20:40,059
and help today's students in school

1941
01:20:40,059 --> 01:20:42,308
resolve some of the dilemmas I faced

1942
01:20:42,308 --> 01:20:44,010
back in my own student days.

1943
01:20:44,610 --> 01:20:46,186
Yes, sometimes I reach out

1944
01:20:46,186 --> 01:20:48,456
and talk with some college students

1945
01:20:48,456 --> 01:20:49,970
still studying in China.

1946
01:20:50,330 --> 01:20:53,410
I noticed that many of these students

1947
01:20:53,410 --> 01:20:56,490
still face the same challenges today

1948
01:20:56,490 --> 01:20:58,536
Twenty years ago, I started college in 2005.

1949
01:20:58,536 --> 01:20:59,784
And now, two decades later,

1950
01:20:59,784 --> 01:21:02,730
many students still face the same dilemmas I did back then.

1951
01:21:02,930 --> 01:21:05,546
so I'm doing quite a few things now to help them out;

1952
01:21:05,546 --> 01:21:07,569
I set up a personal charitable foundation

1953
01:21:07,690 --> 01:21:08,570
And then,

1954
01:21:08,650 --> 01:21:11,548
one of the focuses of the foundation

1955
01:21:11,548 --> 01:21:14,930
is to work with universities across China

1956
01:21:15,450 --> 01:21:17,876
and help college students by offering them

1957
01:21:17,876 --> 01:21:19,668
ample guidance on career paths,

1958
01:21:19,668 --> 01:21:22,540
as well as giving them more courage and resources

1959
01:21:22,540 --> 01:21:23,830
to pursue their goals.

1960
01:21:24,150 --> 01:21:26,435
so they can make more diverse choices

1961
01:21:26,435 --> 01:21:29,030
right at the very start of their journeys

1962
01:21:29,110 --> 01:21:30,230
because we back then were

1963
01:21:30,510 --> 01:21:33,253
on one hand, we lacked information and knew not what to do;

1964
01:21:33,253 --> 01:21:34,766
on the other, when making choices,

1965
01:21:34,766 --> 01:21:36,990
we lacked the courage to take a different path

1966
01:21:37,030 --> 01:21:39,830
in the end, everyone picked the very same path

1967
01:21:39,870 --> 01:21:41,512
but once you joined Meta,

1968
01:21:41,512 --> 01:21:45,070
did you feel you found a way to rediscover yourself?

1969
01:21:45,620 --> 01:21:48,355
just a career where you stand out,

1970
01:21:48,355 --> 01:21:52,699
or perhaps a sacred turning point of sorts, if you will.

1971
01:21:53,060 --> 01:21:55,977
Do you see this as a fortunate outcome,

1972
01:21:55,977 --> 01:21:58,968
or is it something you'd eventually find

1973
01:21:58,968 --> 01:22:00,689
through constant trial?

1974
01:22:00,690 --> 01:22:05,198
because I think many geniuses were extremely outstanding

1975
01:22:05,198 --> 01:22:07,533
and stood out in high school,

1976
01:22:07,533 --> 01:22:10,030
yet later faded into the crowd.

1977
01:22:10,350 --> 01:22:11,374
Yes, I think in life

1978
01:22:11,374 --> 01:22:13,964
many things can't be shaped by so many 'what-ifs,'

1979
01:22:13,964 --> 01:22:15,960
and you don't know what follows them.

1980
01:22:16,280 --> 01:22:18,973
but in objective terms, before joining Meta,

1981
01:22:18,973 --> 01:22:22,630
I indeed made several wrong career choices along the way

1982
01:22:23,470 --> 01:22:24,563
when I joined Meta,

1983
01:22:24,563 --> 01:22:27,660
I had also received offers from a few other places.

1984
01:22:28,060 --> 01:22:29,440
If I hadn't joined Meta

1985
01:22:29,440 --> 01:22:32,320
and had taken one of those other offers instead,

1986
01:22:32,320 --> 01:22:35,320
I think that might have been another wrong choice.

1987
01:22:35,560 --> 01:22:39,960
so I think buying it at that time was quite fortunate indeed.

1988
01:22:40,040 --> 01:22:41,646
How do you define a 'wrong choice'?

1989
01:22:41,646 --> 01:22:43,836
A 'wrong choice' is clearly one you see as such

1990
01:22:43,836 --> 01:22:46,320
in hindsight, though you didn't know it at the time

1991
01:22:46,320 --> 01:22:47,641
Yes, we didn't know back then.

1992
01:22:47,641 --> 01:22:48,871
Yes, even when I was at Meta,

1993
01:22:48,871 --> 01:22:51,083
the few wrong choices I made before joining Meta —

1994
01:22:51,083 --> 01:22:53,019
I didn't know they were wrong at the time.

1995
01:22:53,180 --> 01:22:55,424
you joined AppLovin three years ago

1996
01:22:55,424 --> 01:22:57,220
and rose so swiftly in rank

1997
01:22:57,460 --> 01:22:57,460
Yes.

1998
01:22:57,660 --> 01:22:59,940
Yes, such cases are quite rare in Silicon Valley

1999
01:23:00,180 --> 01:23:02,135
I believe many Chinese engineers

2000
01:23:02,135 --> 01:23:04,580
have quite strong and solid backgrounds

2001
01:23:04,580 --> 01:23:07,026
when they aim to move into leadership roles,

2002
01:23:07,026 --> 01:23:09,700
there are indeed major ceilings they must face.

2003
01:23:09,860 --> 01:23:12,840
Yes, what advice would you offer to them in this case?

2004
01:23:12,840 --> 01:23:15,088
for instance, if he wants to move

2005
01:23:15,088 --> 01:23:17,970
from senior engineer to leadership roles,

2006
01:23:17,970 --> 01:23:19,824
what traits and skill sets

2007
01:23:19,824 --> 01:23:22,820
would help him break through this ceiling?

2008
01:23:23,180 --> 01:23:24,556
I think first and foremost

2009
01:23:24,556 --> 01:23:26,620
is having the right mindset and vision

2010
01:23:26,890 --> 01:23:30,492
many who build careers in big firms

2011
01:23:30,492 --> 01:23:36,050
still seek validation from the system they operate in.

2012
01:23:36,690 --> 01:23:39,330
the big firm system, seeking that system's approval

2013
01:23:39,530 --> 01:23:41,615
for instance, they care deeply about

2014
01:23:41,615 --> 01:23:43,701
what rating they get in the system

2015
01:23:43,701 --> 01:23:45,310
during performance reviews,

2016
01:23:45,310 --> 01:23:48,230
and when the system decides to give them a raise.

2017
01:23:48,350 --> 01:23:50,070
the system sees him as which professional level

2018
01:23:50,310 --> 01:23:51,750
everyone would follow this quite closely.

2019
01:23:51,960 --> 01:23:54,720
but I think if you truly wish to become a leader

2020
01:23:54,950 --> 01:23:56,333
at some stage in your life,

2021
01:23:56,333 --> 01:23:59,511
you should focus more on the standards you set for yourself

2022
01:23:59,511 --> 01:24:00,750
and your self-approval.

2023
01:24:00,990 --> 01:24:03,862
I think once you break free from this mindset,

2024
01:24:03,862 --> 01:24:07,310
it becomes much easier to step into a leadership role?

2025
01:24:07,310 --> 01:24:08,271
once you become a leader,

2026
01:24:08,271 --> 01:24:10,516
the measure of your work is no longer set by the system,

2027
01:24:10,516 --> 01:24:12,200
but by the standards you set for yourself.

2028
01:24:12,400 --> 01:24:13,615
Earlier you mentioned

2029
01:24:13,615 --> 01:24:16,335
growing into leadership—there's a second point

2030
01:24:16,335 --> 01:24:17,840
I'd like to share as well.

2031
01:24:18,130 --> 01:24:19,434
Hmm, in this world

2032
01:24:19,434 --> 01:24:23,810
it seems like there are choices with very high certainty?

2033
01:24:23,810 --> 01:24:26,610
it is well-priced indeed

2034
01:24:27,130 --> 01:24:29,785
when you see an option with very high certainty,

2035
01:24:29,785 --> 01:24:31,820
even if it seems like a good choice,

2036
01:24:31,820 --> 01:24:34,420
the cost you pay afterward will be quite high?

2037
01:24:34,420 --> 01:24:35,433
or the fixed cost you pay

2038
01:24:35,433 --> 01:24:37,380
will yield only limited returns in the long run.

2039
01:24:37,420 --> 01:24:39,918
because a choice with high certainty

2040
01:24:39,918 --> 01:24:42,348
is one whose value is known to all,

2041
01:24:42,348 --> 01:24:44,570
so you'll face more competition?

2042
01:24:44,890 --> 01:24:46,514
so one key point here

2043
01:24:46,514 --> 01:24:50,614
is how you can identify and truly appreciate choices

2044
01:24:50,614 --> 01:24:53,090
that carry a lot of uncertainty.

2045
01:24:53,840 --> 01:24:55,990
when facing uncertain choices,

2046
01:24:55,990 --> 01:24:58,438
you must have a broader mindset —

2047
01:24:58,438 --> 01:25:02,739
treat such choices as an investment, not just a transaction.

2048
01:25:03,020 --> 01:25:05,180
I've spoken with many candidates myself?

2049
01:25:05,380 --> 01:25:07,751
candidates who left a poor impression

2050
01:25:07,751 --> 01:25:11,020
treated this job opportunity as just a transaction

2051
01:25:11,600 --> 01:25:14,632
he might think that joining this firm right now

2052
01:25:14,632 --> 01:25:18,052
means he should get certain things from it right now,

2053
01:25:18,052 --> 01:25:20,440
and thus fail to truly appreciate it.

2054
01:25:20,730 --> 01:25:21,888
this is an investment

2055
01:25:21,888 --> 01:25:24,370
by both the firm and the individual involved

2056
01:25:24,690 --> 01:25:27,831
the company is willing to hand him a major chance

2057
01:25:27,831 --> 01:25:29,370
despite the uncertainty.

2058
01:25:29,740 --> 01:25:32,311
so he is willing, at this point, in this very uncertainty,

2059
01:25:32,311 --> 01:25:34,257
to give the firm the benefit of the doubt —

2060
01:25:34,257 --> 01:25:35,445
that is quite important,

2061
01:25:35,445 --> 01:25:35,819
isn't it?

2062
01:25:36,050 --> 01:25:38,993
seeing a job opportunity as an investment,

2063
01:25:38,993 --> 01:25:41,650
with returns that come in the future ?

2064
01:25:41,650 --> 01:25:43,786
investing—when you invest, in the short term

2065
01:25:43,786 --> 01:25:44,880
it always costs money

2066
01:25:44,960 --> 01:25:46,823
if you spend 100 yuan to invest in something,

2067
01:25:46,823 --> 01:25:47,800
you lose 100 yuan today

2068
01:25:47,880 --> 01:25:50,480
but if you see this chance merely as a transaction

2069
01:25:50,480 --> 01:25:52,261
that if I give you 100 yuan today,

2070
01:25:52,261 --> 01:25:55,020
I must get a 100-yuan return right at that moment

2071
01:25:55,020 --> 01:25:56,687
you are effectively turning away

2072
01:25:56,687 --> 01:25:58,460
many truly precious opportunities.

2073
01:25:58,500 --> 01:26:00,220
That was well said

2074
01:26:00,300 --> 01:26:02,650
And one last point for you folks,

2075
01:26:03,370 --> 01:26:05,915
to those young folks thinking of joining AppLovin,

2076
01:26:05,915 --> 01:26:09,033
or recent grads who are still a bit unsure about their path,

2077
01:26:09,033 --> 01:26:11,320
is there anything you'd like to say to them?

2078
01:26:11,320 --> 01:26:14,200
please encourage folks to apply for this AppLovin role

2079
01:26:14,200 --> 01:26:15,680
when young folks are making their choices

2080
01:26:15,800 --> 01:26:18,895
Hmm, when young folks are making choices, right?

2081
01:26:18,895 --> 01:26:22,610
The simplest pick is to go with the most popular ones.

2082
01:26:22,650 --> 01:26:24,577
the reason it's popular is clear —

2083
01:26:24,577 --> 01:26:26,445
they're rarely the worst options,

